The  $Z^\prime$  couplings to  leptons and to quarks involve the same couple of parameters $\epsilon_1$ and $\epsilon_2$. The consequence is that correlations  can be established  among  observables in the different sectors. Important cases are the correlations among  the LFV  $b \to s \ell_1^+ \ell_2^-$ modes and   the LFV leptonic decays  $\mu^- \to e^- \gamma$ and $\tau^- \to \mu^- \mu^+ \mu^-$.

Figure \ref{Bsvsmueg1000}  shows the correlation between ${\bar {\cal B}}(B_s \to \tau^+ \mu^-)$ and ${\cal B}(\mu^- \to e^- \gamma)$ obtained  for $M_{Z^\prime}=1$ TeV and $3$ TeV. 
The dark points show the results without imposing constraints from LFC mode, the light points the results obtained  imposing that the experimental ${\bar {\cal B}}(B_s \to \mu^+ \mu^-)$ is recovered. The right plots include the upper bound ${\cal B}(\mu^- \to e^- \gamma)<1.5 \times 10^{-13} \,\, (\rm{at}\,  90\% \, {\rm CL})$ recently established by the MEG II Collaboration\cite{MEGnew},   which improves the previous limit ${\cal B}(\mu^- \to e^- \gamma)<4.2 \times 10^{-13} \,\, (\rm{at}\,  90\% \, {\rm CL})$ \cite{MEG:2016leq}. This bound reduces also the room  for ${\bar {\cal B}}(B_s \to \tau^+ \mu^-)$.
The plots are an example of the consequences of the most peculiar feature of the ABCD model, the interplay of quark and lepton observables. Within the model values of ${\cal B}(\mu^- \to e^- \gamma)$ as large as $O(10^{-11})$ can be obtained, however, the requirement that the rate of $B_s \to \mu^+ \mu^-$ agrees with experiment (light yellow points in the plot) suppresses ${\cal B}(\mu^- \to e^- \gamma)$. 

The correlation between ${\cal B}(\bar B^0 \to \bar K^{*0} \tau^+ \mu^-)$ and ${\cal B}(\mu^- \to e^- \gamma)$ is displayed in Fig.~\ref{Kstarvsmueg1000}, and similar remarks hold. In particular,
the  bound on ${\cal B}(\mu^- \to e^- \gamma)$ \cite{MEGnew}    reduces the allowed range for ${\cal B}(B \to K^{*} \tau^+ \mu^-)$, see Table \ref{tabLFV1000}.
The same occurs for $B_s \to \phi \tau^+ \mu^-$, as we report in the same Table.

As for the correlations involving ${\cal B}(\tau^- \to \mu^- \mu^+ \mu^-)$,  the experimental upper bound ${\cal B}(\tau^- \to \mu^- \mu^+ \mu^-)<2.1 \times 10^{-8}$  \cite{Navas:PDG} must be taken into account,
however it does not provide significant constraints for  $B_s$ and $B$ modes, as it can be inferred from  Figs.~\ref{Bsvstau3mu}. 
Accounting for the measurement of the rate of $B_s \to \mu^+ \mu^-$, the largest value for ${\cal B}(\tau^- \to \mu^- \mu^+ \mu^-)$ is about $1.2 \times 10^{-8}$, a relevant result when planning new facilities.
