\section{Conclusions}
The search for BSM effects is currently facing the evidence that deviations from SM predictions exist but  they are small. For example, the flavour anomalies are  below the required level of significance to claim the evidence of new physics.  Being confident that NP exists,   we have pursued the aim of
justifying  the absence of large deviations. We have  scrutinised channels where anomalies have been detected together with  processes strictly forbidden in  SM, namely lepton flavour violating modes, and the correlations among them.

 In the proposed simple extension of the SM,  the ABCD model,  the new gauge boson $Z^\prime$ has 
  flavour conserving and flavour violating generation dependent  couplings to fermions.  The  model is based on a special solution of the gauge anomaly cancellation equations, which relates the quark and lepton couplings to $Z^\prime$. The flavour observables in the two sectors are therefore intertwined,  producing nontrivial correlations.

As  noticed at the formulation of  the model \cite{Aebischer:2019blw}, quark and lepton sectors mutually act to prevent large deviations from  SM. This is a welcome feature in view of the small discrepancies with respect to  SM displayed by measurements of the flavour observables.
Moreover, the possibility of having lepton flavour violation  implies that modes, such as those induced by $b \to s \ell_1^- \ell_2^+$ with $\ell_1 \neq \ell_2$, can occur and are correlated to LFV leptonic modes, such as $\mu^- \to e^- \gamma$ and $\tau^- \to \mu^- \mu^+ \mu^-$, and to the LFC modes $b \to s \ell^- \ell^+$ as well. 
We summarize below our main findings and indicate the corresponding plots.
\begin{itemize}
\item
We have shown that the NP contribution to the Wilson coefficients ${\rm Re}(C_9^{\rm NP})_{\ell \ell}$ and ${\rm Re}(C_{10}^{\rm NP})_{\ell \ell}$, $\ell=\mu,\,\tau$, is $\simeq 10\%$ of the corresponding SM coefficients. The analogous LFV coefficients ${\rm Re}(C_9^{\rm NP})_{\mu \tau}$ and ${\rm Re}(C_{10}^{\rm NP})_{\mu \tau}$ are approximately of the same size as ${\rm Re}(C_{9(10)}^{\rm NP})_{\mu \mu}$, as  shown in Figs.~\ref{Wilson1000}-\ref{Wilsonmix}. 
\item
We have found that LFV modes could reach detectable rates,  probably accessible in the near future, see Figs.~\ref{BsLFV}-\ref{BsphiLFV}.
\item
The role of quark and lepton observables to mutually constrain each other is visible in the results in Table \ref{tabLFV1000} and in Figs.~\ref{Bsvsmueg1000}-\ref{Kstarvsmueg1000}. The experimental upper limit for  ${\cal B}(\mu \to e \gamma)$ efficiently constrains the $B$ and $B_s$ LFV decays.
\item
The  branching ratio of the LFV  $\tau \to 3\mu$ mode can reach values close to the present upper bound, as shown in Fig.~\ref{Bsvstau3mu}.
\end{itemize}
All  findings are  good news for the NP searches  at the   facilities planned within the future strategy for particle physics.

