\section{Mixing of nonlocal gluon and quark operators} \label{2}
Throughout this paper, we focus on the mixing between gluon and quark nonlocal operators, adopting the convention in Ref.~\cite{Yao:2022vtp}
\begin{align} \label{def_op}
{O}_{g,u}(z_1,z_2) &= g^{\mu\nu}_{{\perp}} {\bf F}_{\mu\nu}, \hspace{2em}
{O}_{g,h}(z_1,z_2) =i \epsilon_\perp^{\mu\nu} {\bf F}_{\mu\nu}, \nn\\
{O}_{q}^s(z_1,z_2) &=\frac{1}{2} \left[ O_{q}(z_1,z_2) \mp O_{q}(z_2,z_1) \right], \hspace{2em}
O_{q}(z_1, z_2)=\bar \psi(z_1)\Gamma [z_1, z_2] \psi(z_2),
\end{align}
where ${\bf F}_{\mu\nu} = z_{12}^\rho {\rm F}_{\rho\mu}(z_1) [z_1, z_2]{\rm F}_{\nu\sigma}(z_2) z_{12}^\sigma \equiv\text F_{z_{12}\mu} (z_1) [z_1,z_2] \,\text F_{\nu z_{12}} (z_2)$ with ${\rm F}_{\mu\nu}$ being the gluon field strength tensor. $[z_1, z_2]$ denotes the Wilson line which resides in the adjoint (fundamental) representation for gluon (quark) fields, respectively. $z_{12}^\mu=z_1^\mu-z_2^\mu={\bf{z}}_1 v^\mu-{\bf{z}}_2 v^\mu={\bf{z}}_{12} v^\mu$ can be a lightcone vector (for $v^2=0$) or a spacelike vector (for $v^2=-1$). The subscript $u, h$ denotes the unpolarized and helicity gluon operator. The superscript $s$ in $O_q^s$ indicates the flavor-singlet quark combination. For the unpolarized (helicity) case, one takes the minus (plus) sign in the square bracket. $\Gamma$ is the Dirac structure taken as $\gamma^+  (\gamma^+\gamma_5)$ when $v^2=0$ and $\gamma^t  (\gamma^z\gamma_5)$ when $v^2=-1$ for the unpolarized (helicity) quark operator. The transversity operator is not shown here, as there is no mixing between gluon and quark transversity operators. Note that Bose symmetry indicates that the gluon operators must satisfy certain relations under $z_1\leftrightarrow z_2$. For example, $O_{g,u}(z_1,z_2)$ shall be symmetric, whereas $O_{g,h}(z_1,z_2)$ shall be anti-symmetric under the exchange $z_1\leftrightarrow z_2$. 




The mixing between gluon and quark LF operators has been well studied in their scale evolution~\citep{Balitsky:1987bk}:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:scale}
\mu\frac{d}{d\mu}O_{g}(z_{1},z_{2})=\frac{1}{z_{12}}\int_{0}^{1}du\int_{0}^{1}dv\,\theta(1-u-v)\mathcal{K}_{gq}(u,v)O_{q}^{\text{s}}(z_{12}^{u},z_{21}^{v})+\cdots,
\end{equation}
where $``\cdots"$ denotes the gluon-to-gluon channel contribution and is irrelevant to the discussion in the present work. $z_{i}$ denotes the LF coordinate, and $z^u_{12}=(1-u)z_1+u z_2$. The one-loop results have been available for a long time (see e.g.~\citep {Balitsky:1987bk}). Based on constraints from conformal symmetry, the two-loop evolution kernels are also known~\citep{Belitsky:1999hf,Braun:2019qtp}. Sandwiching the LF operators between non-forward hadron states and performing a Fourier transform, the above equation yields the evolution equation for the gluon GPD:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:LCGPD}
\mu\frac{d}{d\mu}F_{g}(x,\xi,\Delta^{2})=\int_{-1}^{1}dy\,K_{gq}^{\prime}(x,y,\xi)F_{q}^{s}(y,\xi,\Delta^{2})+\cdots.
\end{equation}
where $x,\xi,\Delta^2$ represent the momentum fraction, skewness and total momentum transfer squared, respectively.

Due to the $\frac{1}{z_{12}}$ factor in Eq.\,\eqref{eq:scale} which is singular when $z_{12}\to 0$, the momentum space evolution kernel $K_{gq}^{\prime}$ in Eq.\,\eqref{eq:LCGPD} is not unique. We can formally write
\begin{equation} \label{a}
K_{gq}^{\prime}(x,y,\xi)=-i\int_{a}^{x} dx'K_{gq}(x',y,\xi),
\end{equation}
with
$$K_{gq}(x,y,\xi)=\int_{0}^{1}dudv\,\theta(1-u-v)\mathcal{K}_{gq}(u,v)\delta((1-u-v)y+\xi(v-u)-x)\, ,$$
where $a$ is an arbitrary lower integration limit~\cite{Belitsky:2005qn}. A popular treatment in the literature is to separate $K^{\prime}_{gq}(x,y,\xi)$ into different kinematic regions, and determine $a$ by imposing physical constraints~\citep{Balitsky:1997mj}. For example, in the DGLAP region, we have $y>x>\xi$, thus one can either choose $a=\xi$  or $a=y$.

In the case of quasi-LF operators, the mixing between gluons and quarks is often formulated in the form of a factorization formula~\citep{Yao:2022vtp}:
\begin{equation} \label{origin_coor}
O_g(z_1,z_2)=\frac{1}{z_{12}}\int_{0}^{1} d\alpha d\beta\, \theta(1-\alpha-\beta) C_{gq}(\alpha,\beta,\mu^2 z_{12}^2)O_q^{\text{s, l.t.}}(z_{12}^{\alpha},z_{21}^{\beta})+\cdots,
\end{equation}
where $z_i$ parameterize spacelike vectors, the superscript $``\text{l.t.}"$ denotes the leading-twist projection of the operator.
$C_{gq}(\alpha,\beta,\mu^2z_{12}^2)$ is the matching kernel in coordinate space. It leads to the factorization between gluon quasi-GPD and quark GPD~\citep{Yao:2022vtp,Ma:2022gty}:
\begin{equation} \label{ori_mom}
\mathbb{H}_g(x,\xi,\Delta^2, P^z)=\int_{-1}^{1}dy\,\mathbb{C}_{gq}(x,y,\xi,\frac{\mu}{P^z})H_q^{\text{s}}(y,\xi,\Delta^2,\mu)+\cdots,
\end{equation}
where $\mathbb{H}_g(x,\xi,\Delta^2,P^z)$ is the gluon quasi-GPD and $H_q^{\text{s}}$ is the quark GPD, $\mathbb{C}_{gq}(x,y,\xi,\frac{\mu}{P^z})$ is the matching kernel in momentum space which can be obtained from $C_{gq}$ as follows:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:rela}
\mathbb{C}_{gq}(x,y,\xi,\frac{\mu}{P^z})=\int\frac{dz_{12}}{2\pi}\frac{1}{z_{12}}\int_{\alpha\beta}C_{gq}(\alpha,\beta,\mu^2z_{12}^2),
\end{equation}
where we have used the shorthand notation $$\int_{\alpha\beta}\equiv\int_{0}^{1} d\alpha d\beta\, \theta(1-\alpha-\beta)e^{iz_{12}P^z\big(x+\xi(\alpha-\beta)-(1-\alpha-\beta)y\big)}\, .$$ 
Note that Eq.\,\eqref{eq:rela} involves the Fourier transform of both $\frac{1}{z_{12}}$ and $\frac{\text{ln}z_{12}^2}{z_{12}}$, which, without specifying a proper prescription for the singularity at $z_{12}=0$, will lead to an ambiguous/singular result. One can also calculate the momentum evolution of $\mathbb{C}_{gq}$ as
\begin{equation} \label{evo_FT}
P^{z}\frac{d}{dP^{z}}\mathbb{C}_{gq}(x,y,\xi,\frac{\mu}{P^{z}})=P^{z}\int\frac{dz_{12}}{2\pi}\int_{\alpha\beta} i(x+\xi(\alpha-\beta)-(1-\alpha-\beta)y)C_{gq}(\alpha,\beta,\mu^2z_{12}^2).
\end{equation}
For similar reasons, the result of Eq.\,\eqref{evo_FT} is ambiguous as well. 

From the discussion above, the ambiguity in the mixing channel contribution in momentum space shares a similar origin for both quasi-LF operators and LF operators: the absence of a well-defined prescription for handling the singularity at $z_{12}=0$ in Fourier transform. 
In the following, we will focus on the quasi-LF operators, since once a consistent regularization prescription for the singularity at $z_{12}=0$ is established, the ambiguity in Eq.\,\eqref{a} can be eliminated in the same way, due to the factorization requirement in momentum space: the evolution in $\mu$ must match the evolution in $P^z$.


\section{The role of \texorpdfstring{$\frac{1}{z_{12}}$}{z12} in the Fourier transform} \label{3}
The factorization for the quasi-LF correlator
is derived at a nonzero separation of the gluon and quark fields.
However, as mentioned in the previous section, when Fourier transformed to momentum space, we need a well-defined prescription to regularize the pole at $z_{12}=0$. To illustrate the problem, we compare in Sec.~\ref{SEC:operator} the difference between the traditional operator product expansion (OPE) approach and perturbative factorization in coordinate space. For simplicity, we use the forward gluon correlator as an example. In Sec.~\ref{SEC:MMmatching}, we point out the deficiency of a widely used approach: matching Mellin moments of the matching kernels in coordinate and momentum space.





\subsection{Traditional OPE and factorization in coordinate space}
\label{SEC:operator}
Let us review the traditional OPE of the gluon quasi-LF correlator (forward case): 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:OPEref}
\tilde{h}_{g}(z_{12}^{2},\lambda)=(P^{z})^{2}\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}\frac{(-i\lambda)^{n-2}}{(n-2)!}C_{n-2}^{gq}(\mu^{2}z_{12}^{2})a_{n-1}^{q,s}(\mu)+\cdots,
\end{equation}
with $\lambda=z_{12}P^z$ denoting the quasi-LF distance. The correlator $\tilde{h}_g(z_{12}^2,\lambda)$ is defined as:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{h}_g(z_{12}^2,\lambda)=\big<P\big|O_g(z_1,z_2)\big|P\big>,
\end{equation}
with $O_g(z_1,z_2)$ given in Eq.\,\eqref{def_op}. The polarization vector $\vec S$ of external state is omitted for brevity.
We use the following definitions of quark singlet distribution and moment $a_{n}^{q,s}$:
\begin{align}
f_q^{\text{s}}(y)=&\int\frac{d\lambda_{12}}{2\pi}e^{-i\lambda_{12}P^+y}\big<P\big|O_q^{\text{s}}(\lambda_{12}n,0)\big|P\big>, %\nonumber \\
\qquad
a_{n}^{q,s}=\int_{-1}^{1} dyy^nf_q^{\text{s}}(y).
\end{align}
By defining:$$C_{gq}^{\text{OPE}}(\alpha,\mu^{2}z_{12}^{2})=i\int\frac{d\lambda^{\prime}}{2\pi}e^{i\alpha\lambda^{\prime}}\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}\frac{(-i\lambda^{\prime})^{n-1}}{(n-2)!}C_{n-2}^{gq}(\mu^{2}z_{12}^{2}),$$ we can rewrite Eq.\,\eqref{eq:OPEref} as~\citep{Izubuchi:2018srq,Wang:2019tgg}:
\begin{equation} \label{fact_OPE}
\tilde{h}_{g}(z_{12}^{2},\lambda)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}z_{12}}\int_{-1}^1 d\alpha\, C_{gq}^{\text{OPE}}(\alpha,\mu^{2}z_{12}^{2})h_{q}^{s}(\alpha\lambda)+\cdots,
\end{equation}
where we have used $\lambda=-z_{12}^{-}P^{+}$, with $P^{+}=\sqrt{2}P^z$ in the collinear limit. Making a Fourier transform on both sides of Eq.\,\eqref{fact_OPE}, we obtain the factorization of the gluon quasi-PDF:
\begin{equation} \label{fact_PDF}
x\tilde{f}_{g}(x)=\int_{-1}^{1}dy\,\mathbb{C}_{gq}^{\text{OPE}}(x,y,\frac{\mu}{P^{z}})f_{q}^{\text{s}}(y)+\cdots,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
x\tilde{f}_g(x)=\int\frac{dz_{12}}{2\pi P^z}e^{ixz_{12}P^z}\tilde{h}_g(z_{12}^2,\lambda).
\end{equation}
Thus, we have
\begin{equation} \label{re_OPE}
\mathbb{C}_{gq}^{\text{OPE}}(x,y,\frac{\mu}{P^{z}})=P^z\int\frac{dz_{12}}{2\pi}e^{ixz_{12}P^z}\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}\frac{(-iz_{12}P^z)^{n-2}}{(n-2)!}C_{n-2}^{gq}(\mu^{2}z_{12}^{2})y^{n-1},
\end{equation}
which doesn't include the Fourier transform of $\frac{1}{z_{12}}$. 

On the other hand, for the factorization of the quasi-LF correlator, we have:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:coor_direct}
\tilde{h}_{g}(z_{12}^{2},\lambda)
=\frac{\sqrt{2}P^{z}}{z_{12}}\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}\frac{(-i\lambda)^{n-2}}{(n-2)!}\int_{0}^{1}d\alpha\,\alpha^{n-2}C_{gq}^{\text{coord}}(\alpha,\mu^{2}z_{12}^{2})a_{n-2}^{s}(\mu)+\cdots.
\end{equation}
 In Eq.\,\eqref{eq:coor_direct}, we have used the superscript $\text{``coord''}$ to distinguish the matching kernel from that obtained from OPE:
 $C_{gq}^{\text{OPE}}$.
 For the unpolarized case, the term $\propto\frac{1}{z_{12}}$ in Eq.\,\eqref{eq:coor_direct} vanishes (the first moment of the singlet quark distribution is zero),
 Eq.\,\eqref{eq:OPEref} and Eq.\,\eqref{eq:coor_direct} are thus equivalent. For the polarized case, the equivalence condition is $\int_{0}^{1}d\alpha C_{gq}^{\text{coord}}(\alpha,\mu^2z_{12}^2)=0$. Otherwise, we need to regularize the pole at $z_{12}=0$ in Eq.\,\eqref{eq:coor_direct}. For the nonforward case, the equivalence condition is $\int_{\alpha\beta}C_{gq}^{\text{coord}}(\alpha,\beta,\mu^2z_{12}^2)=0$. 
 The matching kernel $C_{gq}^{\text{coord}}$ can be separated into the evolution part ($C_{gq}^{\text{evol}}$) and the finite part ($C_{gq}^{\text{fin}}$):
\begin{equation} \label{structure}
C_{gq}^{\text{coord}}(\alpha,\mu^2z_{12}^2)=C_{gq}^{\text{fin}}(\alpha)+C_{gq}^{\text{evol}}(\alpha)\text{ln} \left(\frac{z_{12}^2\mu^2}{4e^{-2\gamma_E}}\right).
\end{equation}
From explicit calculations~\citep{Yao:2022vtp}, we find that $C_{gq,h}^{\text{evol}}$ has a vanishing first moment at one-loop:
\begin{equation} \label{first}
\int_{0}^1d\alpha\, C_{gq,h}^{\text{evol}}(\alpha)=0,
\end{equation}
but the first moment of $C_{gq,h}^{\text{fin}}(\alpha)$ is nonzero.

\subsection{Matching of Mellin moments: deficiency}
\label{SEC:MMmatching}
A conventional proposal to remove the ambiguities in the Fourier transform Eq.\,\eqref{eq:rela} is to match Mellin moments of the matching kernel in momentum space with those in coordinate space~\cite{Belitsky:2005qn}:
\begin{equation} \label{matching_master}
\lim\limits_{\xi\to0}\int_0^1dxx^{j}\mathscr{C}_{gq}\left(2\frac{x}{\xi}-1,\frac{2}{\xi}-1\right)=\int_{0}^{1}d\alpha C_{gq}(\alpha,\mu^{2}z_{12}^{2})\frac{-\bar{\alpha}^{j+1}}{j+1},\quad j\geq0\, , 
\end{equation}
where $\bar\alpha = 1-\alpha$. 
Eq.\,\eqref{matching_master} is based on the relation between the evolution kernel in DGLAP and ERBL region (see Eq. (4.93) in \citep{Belitsky:2005qn}). Now, one replaces the r.h.s. with moments of the matching kernel in coordinate space, and solves $\mathscr{C}_{gq}$ on the l.h.s., to all orders in $j$.
Here $\mathscr{C}_{gq}$ can be either the evolution kernel $K_{gq}$ (for GPD)~\citep{Belitsky:2005qn} or the matching kernel (for pseudo-GPD)~\citep{Yao:2022vtp}. 

However, such a method is not sufficient to eliminate all the ambiguities in the Fourier transform, due to the following two facts:
\begin{itemize}
\item The l.h.s. in Eq.\,\eqref{matching_master} can be calculated using Eq.\,\eqref{a} or Eq. (A.2) in~\citep{Yao:2022vtp}, setting the integration constant to zero one gets:
\begin{equation} \label{nonfor-coor}
\lim\limits_{\xi\to0}\int_0^1dxx^{j}\mathscr{C}_{gq}\left(2\frac{x}{\xi}-1,\frac{2}{\xi}-1\right)=\int_{0}^{1}d\alpha C_{gq}(\alpha,\mu^{2}z_{12}^{2})\Big(\frac{-\bar{\alpha}^{j+1}}{j+1}+\frac{1}{j+1}\Big),\quad j\geq0,
\end{equation}
where the second term on the r.h.s. is responsible for the difference between traditional OPE and factorization in coordinate space. Comparing Eq.\,\eqref{nonfor-coor} with Eq.\,\eqref{matching_master}, one finds the matching formula implicitly assumes that the term $\propto\frac{1}{z_{12}}$ (on the r.h.s. of Eq.\,\eqref{eq:coor_direct}) does not contribute to the transformed result, which corresponds to setting a prescription for the pole $\frac{1}{z_{12}}$. However, its physical relevance is yet to be justified.
\item The solution $\mathscr{C}_{gq}$ satisfying Eq.\,\eqref{matching_master} is not unique, since one only fixes the DGLAP region in Eq.\,\eqref{matching_master}. Under the requirement of charge conjugation symmetry, one can easily construct inequivalent solutions at the convolution level.
\end{itemize}



