\section{Regularization prescription for the \texorpdfstring{$\frac{1}{z_{12}}$}{1z12} pole}
\label{4}
The matching kernels in coordinate space contain contributions singular as $z_{12}\rightarrow0$, preventing us from getting a smooth local limit of the factorization. Correspondingly, the first Mellin moments of quasi-PDFs and quasi-GPDs are divergent. These divergences originate from the non-commutativity of the two limits: $P^z\rightarrow\infty$ (in momentum space)/$z_{12}^2\rightarrow0$ (in coordinate space) and $\Lambda_{\rm UV}\rightarrow\infty$. In this section, we show that dimensional regularization (DR) with $d=4-2\epsilon$ can properly treat these divergences, yielding a physically viable prescription for the singularity in practical calculations. We also discuss the principal-value prescription.


\subsection{Recovery of a smooth local limit}


For illustration purposes, we first consider the factorization of forward kinematics in coordinate space at one-loop, in $\overline{\text{MS}}$ scheme. For the unpolarized case, taking the local limit of the factorization Eq.\,\eqref{origin_coor} we get:
$$
\lim_{z_{12}\rightarrow0}\big<P\big|O_{g,u}(z_{12},0)\big|P\big>\!=\!-2ia_{s}C_{F}\lim_{z_{12}\rightarrow0}\int_{0}^{1}d\alpha\left[6\alpha-4+\delta(\alpha)-\text{ln}\left(\frac{z_{12}^2\mu^2}{4e^{-2\gamma_E}}\right)(2\alpha+\delta(\alpha))\right]
$$
\begin{equation} \label{bare_unpol}
\times(1-\alpha)\big<P\big|O_{q,u}^{s,1}(0,0)\big|P\big>,
\end{equation}
where $a_s=\alpha_s/(4\pi)$. 
In the r.h.s. of Eq.\,\eqref{bare_unpol}, we have made a tree-level expansion $O_{q,u}^{\text{s}}(z_{12},0)=z_{12}O_{q,u}^{\text{s},1}(0,0)+\mathcal{O}(z_{12}^3)$, using the fact that $O_{q,u}^{\text{s}}(z_{12},0)$ is \textbf{odd} in $z_{12}$ when sandwiched between identical external states, which is sufficient for our purpose at $\mathcal{O}(\alpha_s)$. $O_{q,u}^{\text{s},1}(0,0)$ is the unpolarized quark operator with one covariant derivative.
The r.h.s. of Eq.\,\eqref{bare_unpol} is at $\mathcal{O}(z_{12}^0)$,
there still remains a term $\propto\text{ln}z_{12}^2$, which is singular as $z_{12}\rightarrow0$. In the spirit of DR, we can make the replacement $$\text{ln}\frac{z_{12}^2\mu^2}{4e^{-2\gamma_E}}\rightarrow\frac{(\frac{z_{12}^2\mu^2}{4e^{-2\gamma_E}})^{\epsilon}-1}{\epsilon}$$
in Eq.\,\eqref{bare_unpol}. Then Eq.\,\eqref{bare_unpol} has a well-defined local limit for positive $\epsilon$
\begin{equation} \label{re_unpol}
\big<P\big|O_{g,u}(0,0)\big|P\big>=-\frac{8ia_{s}C_{F}}{3\epsilon_{\text{UV}}}\big<P\big|O_{q,u}^{s,1}(0,0)\big|P\big>\, .
\end{equation}
Physically, Eq.\,\eqref{re_unpol} is just the mixing of gluon average momentum with quark average momentum. 
The coefficient of the UV pole in Eq.\,\eqref{re_unpol} is the same as that calculated using the local matrix element $\big<P\big|O_{g,u}(0,0)\big|P\big>$.

For the polarized case, $O_{q,h}^{\text{s}}(z_{12},0)$ is \textbf{even} in $z_{12}$ when sandwiched between external states, thus the r.h.s. of Eq.\,\eqref{origin_coor} has a $\frac{1}{z_{12}}$ divergence as $z_{12}\rightarrow0$.
We can use DR to regulate this divergence, the local limit of Eq.\,\eqref{origin_coor} becomes:
\begin{equation} \label{bare_pol}
    \lim_{z_{12}\rightarrow0}\big<P\big|O_{g,h}(z_{12},0)\big|P\big>= \lim_{z_{12}\rightarrow0}\frac{6ia_sC_F(z_{12}^2)^{\epsilon}}{z_{12}}g_A,
\end{equation}
The r.h.s. of Eq.\,\eqref{bare_pol} is free from the logarithmic term since we have used $\int_{0}^1d\alpha \;C_{gq,h}^{\text{evol}}(\alpha)=0,$ prior to taking the local limit. For $\epsilon>\frac{1}{2}$, the r.h.s. is zero and consistent with the l.h.s., using the fact that $O_{g,h}(z_{12},0)$ is \textbf{odd} in $z_{12}$.
The region of $\epsilon$ can be further extended using $$\int\frac{dz_{12}}{2\pi}\frac{d}{dz_{12}}\Big(\frac{(z_{12}^2)^{\epsilon}}{z_{12}}e^{iz_{12}x}\Big)=0\, .$$


.

We then consider the factorization of gluon quasi-PDF in momentum space.
The corresponding matching kernel can be obtained using Eq.\,\eqref{eq:rela} and $C_{gq}$ in $4-2\epsilon$ dimensions:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:reladim}
\mathbb{C}_{gq}(x,y,\xi,\frac{\mu}{P^z},\epsilon)=\int\frac{dz_{12}}{2\pi}\frac{(z_{12}^2)^{\epsilon}}{z_{12}}\int_{\alpha\beta}C_{gq}(\alpha,\beta,\mu^2,\epsilon),
\end{equation}
$\mathbb{C}_{gq}(x,y,\xi,\frac{\mu}{P^z},\epsilon)$ has support $x\in (-\infty,\infty)$.
Expanding it at $\epsilon=0$, we get the matching kernel in 4 dimensions,
which behaves like $x^2$ as $x\rightarrow\infty$.
%The Mellin moments of gluon quasi-PDFs in 4 dimensions are thus divergent.
The matching coefficient functions in momentum space via a direct Fourier transform are thus divergent.
We can consider the matching in $4-2\epsilon$ dimensions instead.  $\mathbb{C}_{gq}(x,y,\xi,\frac{\mu}{P^z},\epsilon)$ in Eq.\,\eqref{eq:reladim} has the following structure:
\begin{align} \label{dim_mom}
\mathbb{C}_{gq}(x,y,\frac{\mu}{P^z},\epsilon)=&a(x,y)\frac1\epsilon\left[1-\left(\frac{4(P^z)^2}{\mu^2}\right)^{-\epsilon}\right] \nonumber\\
&+d(x,y,\epsilon)\big|x-y\big|^{1-2\epsilon}+e(x,y,\epsilon)\big|x\big|^{1-2\epsilon}+f(x,y,\epsilon)\big|x-y\big|^{-2\epsilon},
\end{align}
where $d,e,f$ are polynomials in $x,y,\epsilon$. 
Taking the infinite momentum limit,
the $P^z$-dependence in Eq.\,\eqref{dim_mom} disappears.
We can then calculate the second moment of $\tilde{f}_g(x,\infty)$ in $4-2\epsilon$ dimensions, the results read:
\begin{align} \label{moment_mom}
\big<P\big|O_{g,u}(0,0)\big|P\big>&=-\frac{8ia_sC_F}{3\epsilon_{\text{UV}}}\big<P\big|O_{q,u}^{s,1}(0,0)\big|P\big>,\quad\text{for the unpolarized case}, \nonumber \\
0&=0,\quad\text{for the polarized case}.
\end{align}
Eq.\,\eqref{moment_mom} is consistent with the local limit (OPE) of spacelike correlators, with DR adopted. 
The second line in Eq.\,\eqref{dim_mom} can be written as a sum of dimensionless integrals after proper shifts of the integration variable $x$, making the integrals vanish in DR. 
Thus, the moment of the finite term in Eq.\,\eqref{dim_mom} is zero.

The above discussions focus on the forward correlators and quasi-PDFs. For the nonforward correlators and quasi-GPDs, employing DR, one can correctly reproduce the mixing patterns of local nonforward matrix elements, consistent with the results via direct calculations.

In lattice calculations of nonlocal correlators, the results are smooth when approaching the local limit ($z_1\to z_2$). Since here one implicitly includes contributions from all orders in $\alpha_s$, the singular term like $\ln z_{12}^2$ in fixed-order perturbation theory is effectively resummed, giving a polynomial structure in $z_{12}$~\citep{Su:2022fiu}. In this sense, DR is also consistent with the lattice extraction of parton distributions.


\subsection{Comparison of two methods for calculating \texorpdfstring{$\mathbb{C}_{gq}$}{Cgq}}
In the previous subsection, we have shown that DR yields a consistent regularization prescription for the $1/z_{12}$ pole. We denote the expansion of $\mathbb{C}_{gq}(x,y,\xi,\frac{\mu}{P^z},\epsilon)$ (in Eq.\,\eqref{eq:reladim}) at $\epsilon=0$ as $\mathbb{C}_{gq}^{\text{FT,DR}}$.
Another method to calculate $\mathbb{C}_{gq}$ is to first Fourier transform the amplitude to momentum space, which implicitly includes the contribution at $z_{12}=0$, then work out the loop integral in momentum space~\citep{Ma:2022gty,Wang:2017qyg}, this results are denoted as $\mathbb{C}_{gq}^{\text{mom}}$.


In Table \ref{Tab:comparison_directFT}, we compare the two sets of matching kernels at one-loop: $\mathbb{C}_{gq}^{\text{FT,DR}}$ and $\mathbb{C}_{gq}^{\text{mom}}$ (in~\citep{Ma:2022gty}), of quasi-PDFs and quasi-GPDs. They are not the same superficially, but the differences don't contribute to the convolution due to the symmetry properties of quark singlet distributions (in Eq.\,\eqref{ori_mom} and Eq.\,\eqref{fact_PDF}). 
The gluon-in-quark mixing channel receives contributions from two diagrams, which are crossed to each other. In coordinate space, integration-by-parts (IBPs) over the Feynman parameters $\alpha,\beta$ is used to eliminate certain contributions incompatible with the factorization theorem (see Eq.\,(3.10)---(3.12) in~\citep{Yao:2022vtp}). For a specific diagram, making a Fourier transform of the direct diagrammatic coordinate space result \textit{before} the IBPs (Eq.\,(3.10) in~\citep{Yao:2022vtp}), we can arrive at an expression exactly the same as that directly calculated in momentum space.



