Alternatively, one may think of using the principal-value prescription to regulate the poles at $z_{12}=0$ in Eq.\,\eqref{bare_unpol} and Eq.\,\eqref{bare_pol}. 
It is worth pointing out that at one-loop, the $\mathbb{C}_{gq}$ calculated using principal-value prescription: 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:relaPV}
\mathbb{C}_{gq}^{\text{FT,PV}}(x,y,\xi,\frac{\mu}{P^z})=\int\frac{dz_{12}}{2\pi}\text{P}\Big[\frac{1}{z_{12}}\int_{\alpha\beta}C_{gq}(\alpha,\beta,\mu^2z_{12}^2)\Big],
\end{equation}
agrees with $\mathbb{C}_{gq}^{\text{FT,DR}},$ for all types of quasi distributions. The manipulation $``\text{P}\left[f(z_{12})\right]"$ means subtracting the point at $z_{12}=0$ in $f(z_{12})$.
But unlike DR, in some cases, %factorization 
 convoluting the LF operator with the coefficient function obtained using the principal-value prescription gives inconsistent results in the local limit.  Employing the factorization theorem for the polarized forward correlator, and using principal-value prescription to obtain the matching coefficient function, one gets a consistent result %$0=0$ 
in the local limit.
However, the factorization theorem for the polarized nonforward correlator implies the following local limit:
\begin{align} \label{nonforward-pol}
0=&\int_0^1d\alpha\int_0^{1-\beta}C_{gq}(\alpha,\beta,\mu^2z_{12}^2)(1-\alpha+\beta)\big<P'\big|O_{q,h}^{\text{s,1}}(0,0)\big|P\big> \nonumber \\
=&\;6ia_sC_F\big<P'\big|O_{q,h}^{\text{s,1}}(0,0)\big|P\big>,
\end{align}
if principal-value prescription is used. Eq.\,\eqref{nonforward-pol} 
is evidently inconsistent as 
$$\langle P'\big|O_{q,h}^{\text{s,1}}(0,0)\big|P\rangle \neq 0\, .$$
Moreover, in momentum space, we haven't found a direct implementation of principal-value prescription, such that gluon quasi-PDFs and GPDs have finite first moments.

From Table \ref{Tab:comparison_directFT}, we see that the scale dependence of $\mathbb{C}_{gq}^{\text{FT,DR}}$ is the same as that of $\mathbb{C}_{gq}^{\text{mom}}$. This resolves the ambiguity related to the choice of the lower limit $a$ in Eq.\,\eqref{a}. For the polarized case, due to symmetry constraints, the integration constant in Eq.\,\eqref{a} must be zero, which gives the same result as that calculated in momentum space.  

In a recent work~\citep{Yao:2022vtp}, the mixing channel contributions of gluon quasi-GPDs and PDFs are conveniently determined by matching Mellin moments in coordinate and momentum space. The results turn out to be equivalent to those obtained in the present work, except for the finite term in the gluon-in-quark mixing channel of the gluon helicity quasi-GPD and quasi-PDF, which requires a minor revision. 
For calculations of the gluon-in-quark channel in momentum space, one can refer to~\citep{Ma:2022gty}(for quasi-GPDs and quasi-PDFs) and~\citep{Wang:2019tgg}(for quasi-PDFs), where the results are consistent with the conclusion in this work.




\section{Conclusion} \label{5}
In this work, we present a systematic investigation of the mixing between nonlocal gluon operators and flavor-singlet quark operators for both lightlike and spacelike separations. Our analysis reveals that dimensional regularization provides a physically viable prescription for regularizing the pole at zero field separation, which also reproduces the expected mixing pattern of local operators. Through explicit calculations, we demonstrate that the coordinate space factorization, when treated with DR and Fourier transformed to momentum space, yields results consistent with those obtained from direct calculations in momentum space\footnote{(i.e., it is necessary to Fourier transform the general $ d$-dimensional coordinate space coefficient function and then perform the $\epsilon$ expansion at the very last step)}. Our work eliminates a long-standing conundrum in relating coordinate space and momentum space results for nonlocal operator mixing, and is expected to have a significant impact on lattice calculations of gluon PDFs (GPDs) and singlet quark PDFs (GPDs). 
