In both physics and computer science, vibration modes in disordered systems and the spectral behavior of graph neural networks (GNNs) exhibit similarities in their structures and behaviors. The analysis of vibration modes in disordered systems is crucial for understanding the properties of materials, especially in irregular structures or materials like glass. In the spectral behavior of graph neural networks, nodes and edges represent the entities and their relationships in the graph, similar to how particles and their interactions are modeled in disordered systems. By drawing analogies between these two domains, we can better understand their deep connections and apply this understanding to the design and optimization of graph neural networks.

