Thus, we can find a countable subset
$D_{r} \subset T$ such that
%$$\{x\in X\mid f(x) >r\}
%\subset \bigcup_{t\in
%{D_{r}}}
%\{x\in X\mid f_t(x)>r\}\subset \bigcup_{t\in D}\{x\in X\mid f_t(x)>r\},$$
$U\subset\bigcup_{t\in{D_{r}}}U_t \subset\bigcup_{t\in{D}}U_t$,
where $D$ is the union of
$D_{r}$ over all rational $r$.
Thus, $D$ is countable and, for any $x\in X$ and rational $r<f(x)$, we have $\sup_{t\in D}f_t(x)>r$.
The density of the rational numbers in $\mathbb{R}$ entails $f(x)\le\sup_{t\in D}f_t(x)$.
