\section{Conclusions}\label{sec:conclusions}

\begin{comment}
We have considered a general infinite convex optimization problem, and developed a methodology for closing the duality gap and
guaranteeing strong duality.
Application of the fuzzy
multiplier rule
for infinite sums has produced
general
optimality conditions/multiplier
%\red{subdifferential}
rules for such problems.
%\AK{24/06/25.
%To be expanded.}
\end{comment}

In this work, we have explored new duality perspectives for infinite optimization problems with infinitely many constraints in a Banach space. Classical duality schemes based on Haar duality often fail to ensure strong duality. To address this issue, we have 
%focused on 
studied broader Lagrangian-type dual formulations that incorporate the concept of infinite sum for arbitrary collections of functions introduced in \cite{HanKruLop1}. We have proposed two new dual problems: the first one, denoted (\ref{problemd}), is constructed directly from the original data of problem (\ref{problemp}); the second involves a Lagrangian that includes both the infinite sum of the constraint functions and a limit function, reflecting the role of compactification techniques used in our approach. 
We have shown that under the standard Slater condition these dual formulations are better suited to guarantee zero duality gap and strong duality (existence of dual solutions). Furthermore, we have established general optimality conditions (in the form of multiplier rules) for problem (\ref{problemp}) by applying a subdifferential calculus rule from \cite{HanKruLop1}.


