\section{Results and Discussion}
\label{sec:results}
%
%
In this section, we apply the inexact Lanczos method using TTNSs to various challenging molecules.
To simplify the discussion, in the following, the range of $\hat H$ is zero-point-energy adjusted such that the ground state has an energy of $0$.
To show the general convergence behavior, we first target a few states of \ce{CH3CN} using both initial DMRG-based guesses and random guesses (\autoref{sec:ch3cn_specific_state}).
This is followed by targeting the lowest 58 %
eigenstates and 
26 %
eigenstates in the interval from $2572$ to $\unit[2777]{\icm}$ %
of \ce{CH3CN} (\autoref{sec:ch3cn_all_states}). This includes protocols how to perform multiple Lanczos runs to compute all states in one interval. 
We then compute Fermi resonance states of the Zundel ion (\autoref{sec:zundel}) and selected states of the Eigen ion (\autoref{sec:eigen}).

Throughout, the energies we show are the eigenvalues of the Hamiltonian represented in the TTNS Lanczos basis. That is, the energies belong to eigenvectors that are linear combinations of TTNSs. We do not show the less accurate energies after summing up TTNSs in line \ref{line:construct_eigenstates} in Algorithm \autoref{algo:lanczos}, as this summation can be done to arbitrary accuracy, if needed.  
In many cases, however, using the same $\bdimmax$ for the fitting as for the inexact Lanczos computations, and a relative convergence tolerance of $10^{-9}$, the fitting leads to energy changes that are lower than the eigenvalue error. 

We now discuss some simulation parameters we used throughout, unless mentioned otherwise. 
We used a Krylov space dimension of $\Nkrylov=10$ with a relative eigenvalue convergence of $\tolkrylov=10^{-6}$. 
%
To solve the linear system for each tensor, \autoref{eq:linear},
we used the  GCROT solver with an absolute (relative) convergence tolerance  of $10^{-9}$ ($10^{-2}$),
but the total number of iterations was capped to $10$. 
Otherwise, default parameters were used. 
For state shifting (only applies to \ce{CH3CN}), we set the shift $S$ from  \autoref{eq:H_shifting} to $S=\unit[8499]{\icm}$.
For each sweep-based TNS algorithm, we adapted the bond dimension 
using an SVD threshold of $\epsSVD=5\cdot 10^{-9}$ (see \autoref{sec:ttns}). To avoid bond dimension oscillations, after the bond dimension adaption we increased $\bdim$ to $\bdim +2$.\cite{Computing2019larsson} 
For the approximate orthogonalization (see \autoref{sec:ortho}), we used the same bond dimension parameters and a relative convergence tolerance of $10^{-2}$ with a maximum sweep  number of $40$.


%
\subsection{Initial benchmark: \ce{CH3CN}}
\label{sec:ch3cn_specific_state}
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
\ce{CH3CN} is a common benchmark problem, and we recently used it to compute up to 5000 eigenstates with an error estimate below $\unit[0.0007]{\icm}$.\cite{Benchmarking2025larsson}
Our used PES is based on a quartic force field,\cite{Calculations2005begue} in the parametrization from \lit{Using2011avila}, which is available in the supplementary information of \lit{Benchmarking2025larsson}.
We used the same tree structure as \lit{Benchmarking2025larsson}, which is depicted in \autoref{fig:sweep}.
The kinetic energy operator was based on the simplified $J=0$ Hamiltonian in normal coordinates, $- 0.5 \sum_\kappa \omega_\kappa\ \partial^2/ \partial \hat q_\kappa^2$, 
where $\hat q_\kappa$ is the position operator of mode $\kappa$ and $\omega_\kappa$ is its angular frequency.


We apply our method to target five different energy levels, which can be either non-degenerate or degenerate. 
For this initial test, we used $\sigma$ values that are very close to reference energies. 
The $\sigma$ values are the following: $360$ (state numbers 2,3), $900$ (7), $1259$ (14,15),
$1786$ (39), and $2066$ (57,58) $\unit{\icm}$.
These values are selected based on different regions in the energy spectrum with different state densities. 


Both DMRG and random guesses were based on $\bdimmax=3$.
We used a fixed number of $5$ sweeps to solve the linear system. %
See the Supplementary Information (SI) for the target energies of the DMRG-based guess. 
For using this guess in the inexact Lanczos method, we immediately increased the maximum bond dimension to $\bdimmax=25$.
%
%
However,
for the random guess, we gradually increased $\bdimmax$ 
and tightened $\tolkrylov$
in three stages:
%
%
(1) $\bdimmax=3$ and $\tolkrylov=10^{-3}$; (2) $\bdimmax=10$ and $\tolkrylov=10^{-4}$; (3) $\bdimmax=25$ and $\tolkrylov=10^{-6}$.
We used the block-Lanczos approach for the degenerate states.
%
%

The convergence for the random guess is shown in \autoref{fig:ch3cn_specific_random},
which also displays the reference energies from \lit{Benchmarking2025larsson}.
To keep the comparison simple, here we only compare one single energy level for each target value $\sigma$, but there are other converged states close by.
Note that we show here cumulative iterations, which include restarts with larger bond dimensions for the random guess. 
The number of iterations for each stage is listed in the SI.
For all targets, the random guesses require 7 to 11  iterations to reach energy errors below $\unit[100]{\icm}$. 
The states for $\sigma=\unit[360]{\icm}$ and $\sigma=\unit[2066]{\icm}$ converge after 16 and 17 iterations, respectively,  whereas
the two other states show more erratic convergence behaviors, root-flipping, and do not converge even  after 36 iterations (not shown). 

The convergence dramatically changes once the DMRG guess is used, as shown in \autoref{fig:ch3cn_specific_dmrg}.
%
Selecting the energy with the lowest error for degenerate pairs,
four out of the five states converge after  up to 4
iterations with an error below $\unit[0.007]{\icm}$. The state for $\sigma=\unit[2066]{\icm}$ converges after 7 iterations with the lowest error of  $\unit[0.03]{\icm}$. The degeneracy error, however, is as large as $\unit[0.16]{\icm}$ for $\sigma=\unit[2066]{\icm}$. 
Compared to the random guess, 
the fast convergence is also reflected in the runtimes, as 
the DMRG-based guesses have a runtime that often is two orders of magnitude faster, even though the random guess starts with smaller bond dimensions. 
In both cases, however, it can happen that during the sweep-based optimization of \autoref{eq:SI_system}, the solution oscillates or diverges after some iterations. Particularly, this can happen in the first Lanczos iteration, when the initial guess is poor and solutions are accurate to only $\unit[\sim 10]{\icm}$. In the following iterations, convergence frequently improves to $\unit[\sim0.1]{\icm}$. 
For these reasons, it is important to fix the total number of sweeps to low values of $\mathcal O(10)$. %
This works in most cases and gives reasonable, inexact approximations of \autoref{eq:SI_system}.

