\subsection{Computing all states within an energy interval: \ce{CH3CN}}
\label{sec:ch3cn_all_states}
%
%
%
%
%
%
A common scenario is to compute all eigenstates within a given energy window. To demonstrate that this is possible with the TTNS inexact Lanczos approach, we compute two different energy regions. The first region covers all %
58 %
low-energy eigenstates up to an excitation energy of
$\unit[2065]{\icm}$. %
The second region covers  %
26 %
states between 
$\unit[2572]{\icm}$ and $\unit[2777]{\icm}$. %
The reason for this energy interval is that 
our previous high-accuracy benchmark on \ce{CH3CN} revealed that many eigenstate methods have difficulties in converging the second region.\cite{Benchmarking2025larsson}

To achieve the computation of all states within an energy interval, we not only need multiple inexact Lanczos computations, but  we also need to ensure that we don't miss eigenstates. We approached this quest by using two stages based on TTNSs with different bond dimensions in each stage. The first stage is about ensuring that all states within an interval are computed using low-$\bdimmax$ TTNSs. These TTNSs are then refined/re-optimized using larger $\bdimmax$ in the second stage. The refinement can be repeated by successively increasing $\bdimmax$ until the required accuracy is achieved. 
The two stages are described below. 
Note that we did not strictly follow the parameters mentioned below, 
as the proposed procedure is not fully black-box. Additional details and a few exceptions with slightly different parameters are found in the SI.


%
\subsubsection{Finding all states in one interval}
\label{sec:ch3cn_all_states_in_one_interval}
%
%
%
%

To initialize our procedure to find all states, we set $\sigma$ to the lowest energy of the interval. From each inexact Lanczos computation, we considered not only the eigenstate close to $\sigma$ but all  states
where the summed squared overlap to already computed eigenstates was less than $0.06$, and where 
the norm of the eigenstate residual was less than $\unit[95]{\icm}$ ($\unit[150]{\icm}$) in the low-energy (high-energy) region.
Note that the residual computation is approximate (see \autoref{sec:implementation}), so this 
heuristic parameter does not reflect the actual accuracy, which is much higher.
Further, three outliers with residual norms between $97$ and $\unit[172]{\icm}$ were added to the list of eigenstates in the low-energy region. 
To double-check that no eigenstates have been missed and to retrieve degenerate eigenpairs, in the next computation we set $\sigma$ to the lowest eigenvalue of the previously retrieved eigenstates and shift those in energy (see below).
As new TTNS guess, we used one of the approximate eigenstates with large residual, typically the one closest in energy to the target. %
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
We repeated this until no additional states have been found, and then performed an additional check,
where we again set $\sigma$ to the lowest eigenvalue of previously found states
but use a DMRG-guess.
In some cases, this is important to find states belonging to different irreps.
%
Once we were sure that no eigenstate was missed at one $\sigma$ value, we increased $\sigma$ to the smallest energy of the approximate states with large residual norm that have not yet been added to our list of eigenstates. 
We used the corresponding TTNS as initial guess for the next computation.
%
In addition, to avoid converging to previously found states, in all Lanczos optimizations, we shifted the 10 eigenstates closest to $\sigma$.
For the high-energy region, we shifted 20 states.
The shifting was also used for generating the  DMRG-based guess.
We iterated this procedure until all eigenstates were retrieved.

While the procedure requires repeated computations around the same target energy, the computational cost is modest as the bond dimension can  be set to low values.
Specifically, we used $\bdimmax=10$ for the first (low-energy) energy interval,
 and $\bdimmax=20$  for the second (high-energy) energy interval. %
%
%
To solve \autoref{eq:SI_system}, we used  up to $5$ sweeps with
a relative energy convergence criterion of $5\cdot{10^{-2}}$.


%
%
%
%
%

%
%
%
%
%
