%
\subsubsection{State refining}
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
After the retrieval of the complete list of approximate eigenstates, 
we increased the max.~bond dimension and refined the states. 
To deal with near-degeneracies, here we used the block inexact Lanczos approach. We grouped all eigenstates based on how energetically close they are and used them as initial guess, setting $\sigma$ to the median of the eigenvalues in one block.
%
%
%
We used this block refinement repeatedly, and, for each iteration, we increased the bond dimension in increments of $10$ until $\bdimmax=40$ was reached.
The eigenstates were grouped in intervals such that the maximum block size was kept below $\Nblock=7$. 
We used smaller energy intervals and thus smaller block sizes for states with larger bond dimensions, because these are more accurate and thus expose degeneracies well.
In some cases where the refined states of one block had summed squared overlaps larger than $\sim 0.1$ to a previously computed block, we combined the two blocks and repeated the computation. 
%
This did not increase the maximum block size of $7$. %
For the more difficult high-energy region, we tightened the GCROT relative tolerance to $10^{-4}$ with up to $20$ iterations, and used a fixed number of $5$ sweeps to solve the linear system.

