%	Let $G$ denote a very general closed fiber of $g$, and let $h \colon G \to A$ denote the induced map (it is then automatically generically finite). By definition, we have a diagram as follows: \[ \begin{tikzcd}
%		& G \arrow[ld, "\alb_G"'] \arrow[rd, "h"] &         \\
%		\Alb(G) \arrow[rr] &                         & A.
%	\end{tikzcd} \] Since $\Pic^0(A)$ has a countable dense subset (all its torsion points), $k$ is uncountable and $G$ is very general, this shows that \[ h^* \colon \Pic^0(A) \to \Pic^0(G) \] is also the trivial map. Equivalently, this shows that $\Alb(G) \to A$ is constant. Since $G \to A$ is generically finite, this forces $\dim(G) = 0$.
