\subsection{Hard-core bosons}

If the limit $U\rightarrow\infty$ is taken in the Bose-Hubbard Hamiltonian~(\ref{eqn:bose_hubbard}), we acquire the so-called \textit{hard-core boson} model. At half-filling ($\mu=0$), this model is particle-hole symmetric, meaning that the parameter $\nu$ appearing in Eq.~(\ref{eqn:delta_ns_final}) is identically zero. Unlike the soft-core bosons, the parameter $\gamma$ is not negligible in the hard-core system. We extract $\gamma$ by fitting a quadratic to $n_s(n)$ data as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:hc_gamma}, yielding $\gamma=-2.14(1)ta^4$ when extrapolating to $T=0$. Using the estimators in Sec.~\ref{sec:Num}, we compute for the hard-core system $c=2.2688(3)ta$ and $\varkappa=0.10476(1)t$. 

