A direct relationship between the low-temperature depletion problem and its exact pseudo-classical $(d+1)$-dimensional counterpart is straightforwardly established by using Popov's hydrodynamic action in terms of the field $\Phi$ (see, e.g., \cite{book}):
\be
 S_{\rm hd}[\Phi] \, =\,  \int_0^{\beta} d\tau \! \int \!  d^d r \,  p_0 \left( \mu -   i\dot{\Phi} , \, {\bf v}_s= { \nabla \Phi\over m} \right)  .
\label{S_hydro}
\ee
Here $p_0(\mu, {\bf v}_s)$ is the pressure as a function of $\mu$ and ${\bf v}_s$ (in the reference frame where normal velocity is zero).

Expanding in powers of $\dot{\Phi}$ and $\nabla \Phi$ up to quartic terms, we get
\bea
{\cal L} \, =\, &-&{n_s^{(0)} \over 2} (\nabla \Phi)^2 \, -\, {\varkappa \over 2}\dot{\Phi}^2 \, -\, {\tilde{\sigma} \over 4} (\nabla \Phi)^4 \nonumber \\
 &+&\, {\varkappa^2\gamma \over 2} \dot{\Phi}^2 (\nabla \Phi)^2 \, +\, i{\varkappa \nu \over 2} \dot{\Phi} \,(\nabla \Phi)^2 \label{action_phase} \\  
 &+&\, i\lambda_1 \dot{\Phi}^3 \, + \, \lambda_2 \dot{\Phi}^4\, . ~\nonumber
\eea
Here 
\be
\tilde{\sigma} \, =\, -2 {\partial^2 p_0(\mu, k_0^2)\over \partial(k_0^2)^2}\bigg|_{k_0=0} \, =\,  {\partial n_s(\mu, k_0^2)\over \partial (k_0^2)}\bigg|_{k_0=0}
\ee
[we use convenient variable $k_0$ in a direct analogy with relations (\ref{eqn:nu})] is different from
\be
\sigma \, =\, {\partial n_s (n, k_0^2)\over \partial (k_0^2)}\bigg|_{k_0=0}
\ee
and thus needs to be related to $\sigma$ and other previously introduced parameters. This is conveniently done with the Jacobian technique:
\be
{\partial n_s (n, k_0^2)\over \partial (k_0^2)}  =  {{\cal D} (n_s,\mu)\over {\cal D}(k_0^2,\mu)}  = {{\cal D} (n_s,\mu)\over {\cal D}(k_0^2, n)} \left[ { {\cal D}(k_0^2,\mu)} \over {\cal D}(k_0^2, n)\right]^{-1} \!\! .
\ee
Observing/recalling that
\[
{\partial n_s(n, k_0^2) \over \partial n}\bigg|_{k_0=0}= \, \nu \, ,  \qquad {\partial n_s(n, k_0^2) \over \partial (k_0^2)}\bigg|_{k_0=0}= \, \sigma \, , 
\]
\bea
{\partial \mu (n, k_0^2) \over \partial (k_0^2)}\bigg|_{k_0=0} &=& {\partial^2 {\cal E} (n,k_0^2) \over \partial n \,  \partial (k_0^2)} \bigg|_{k_0=0} \nonumber \\
&=& {1\over 2}{\partial n_s (n,k_0^2) \over  \,  \partial n} \bigg|_{k_0=0}  \, =\, \, {\nu\over 2} \, , \nonumber
\eea
\[
{\partial \mu (n, k_0^2) \over \partial n}\bigg|_{k_0=0} = \, \varkappa^{-1}\, , 
\]
\[
\left[ { {\cal D}(k_0^2,\mu)} \over {\cal D}(k_0^2, n)\right]^{-1} \, =\, {\partial n(\mu, k_0^2)\over \partial \mu} \bigg|_{k_0=0}= \, \varkappa \, ,
\]
we conclude that 
\be
\tilde{\sigma} \, =\, \sigma - \varkappa \nu^2/2 \, .
\ee

{\color{blue} As long as we are concerned with the depletion problem, the terms in the last line of Eq.~(\ref{action_phase}) are irrelevant (subleading).} 
%However, they are of interest in the context of mapping the action onto the simple classical model (\ref{eqn:classical_action}).
{\color{red} Boris: Wrong statement!  The first term in the third line is actually not subleading. In combination with the last term in the second line, it generates the correlator  
\[
\lambda_1 \varkappa \nu \int d^dr\,  d\tau \, \langle \, \dot{\Phi}^3 (0,0) \,  \dot{\Phi} ({\bf r}, \tau) \,  \rangle \, .
\]
An important detail: parameter $\lambda_1$ is not a part of our constant-$n$ theory.  This parameter (proportional to the second derivative of the density with respect to $\mu$ at $T=0$)  should also be relevant to the theory of thermal depletion of the total density at constant $\mu$.}

It is also worth noting that the first two lines can be obtained by integrating out the variable $\eta$ in the action   (\ref{Popov})---quadratic in $\eta$---with the subsequent omission of the subleading terms of gradient expansion.

Comparing (\ref{action_phase}) to the classical model (\ref{eqn:classical_action}), we see two characteristic features the most prominent one being the appearance of imaginary terms. Especially important is the $\nu$-term as it is responsible for the phonon-wind result by Landau. Less dramatic but still important feature is the special role of the imaginary-time direction. Along with quantitative difference with the spatial directions resulting in the anisotropy in the Euclidean spacetime, the imaginary-time direction is characterized by the parity breaking, which, in the language of underlying quantum field theory, originates from the absence of particle-hole symmetry (or the very notion of holes). 
Note also that the $\tau$-parity-breaking terms are imaginary and visa versa---the imaginary terms break parity. 
In the systems where the particle-hole symmetry is enforced microscopically (hard-core bosons at half filling) or emerges in a certain limit (the quantum-rotor model), these terms are identically equal to zero. Here the action becomes real and the only remaining (minor) difference with (\ref{eqn:classical_action}) is due to the spacetime anisotropy. This difference can be completely removed by fine-tuning the parameters $\sigma$, $\gamma$, and $\lambda_2$.

Apart from being imaginary and $\tau$-parity-breaking, the $\nu$-term brings a new mathematical aspect absent in the simple classical model (\ref{eqn:classical_action}). As opposed to the $\gamma$- and $\sigma$-terms that are quartic in $\Phi$, the $\nu$-term is cubic. While the contributions of all the three terms are of the same order of magnitude, the field-theoretical structure of these contributions is qualitatively different. The $\gamma$- and $\sigma$-contributions are expressed in terms of local  observables while the $\nu$-contribution crucially depends on non-local spacetime correlations; see  Eq.~(\ref{K_reduced}).

