\title{Optimal Auction Design for Dynamic Stochastic Environments:  Myerson Meets Naor\thanks{We are grateful to Omar Besbes, Simon Board, Faruk Gul, Navin Kartik,  Jinwoo Kim, Kyungmin Kim, Andreas Kleiner, Fuhito Kojima, Qingmin Liu, Will Ma, George Mailath, Pietro Ortoleva, Alessandro Pavan, Larry Samuelson, Ali Shourideh,  Bruno Strulovici, Olivier Tercieux, Kaizheng Wang, Assaf Zeevi, and participants at Chung-Ang, CMU-Pitt, Columbia IEOR-DRO, HKUST, KAIST, Northwestern, Penn, Princeton, Tokyo, Toronto, and Western seminars, and at  Asia-Pacific Industrial Organization Conference, BU Conference in Honor of Bart Lipman,  Marketplace Innovation Workshop, Michigan-MSU, Online Stochastic Matching (CNRS), POMS-HK, and SITE. We acknowledge research assistance from Mingeon Kim.}}




\author{ Yeon-Koo Che \\ Columbia University
\and Andrew B. Choi \\ University of Michigan
}
\date{May 2025 
}
\maketitle



\begin{abstract}
Allocation of goods and services often involves both stochastic supply and stochastic demand. Motivated by applications such as cloud computing, gig platforms, and blockchain auctions, we study the design of optimal selling mechanisms in an environment where buyers with private valuations arrive stochastically and are assigned goods that also arrive stochastically, and either buyers or goods can be held in a queue at costs until allocation.  The optimal mechanism dynamically leverages competitive pressure across time by managing the queue of buyers and inventory of goods, using reserve prices that increase with the number of buyers in the queue and decrease with the number of items in inventory, and an auction to allocate the goods.



\vspace{2 mm}
\noindent \emph{Keywords}: dynamic auction, queueing, revenue management, steady-state mechanism design
\end{abstract}



\section{Introduction}
 Classic auction models, pioneered by \cite{myerson1981} and \cite{milgrom1982theory}, focus on scenarios where a fixed number of goods are sold to a fixed number of buyers, all simultaneously present.  In contrast, many real-world allocation problems are dynamic and subject to stochastic supply and demand.   
 This is increasingly pronounced in the modern economy, where platforms match demand and supply that arrive asynchronously and stochastically.  
In cloud computing platforms like Amazon's AWS, available capacity can vary due to unpredictable completion times of existing jobs or unforeseen service disruptions, and the demand fluctuates as new tasks arrive. Gig economy platforms like TaskRabbit experience variability in both the availability of service providers and the demand for their services.  Blockchains must allocate randomly arriving transactions to blocks generated at random intervals. Even traditional markets like allocation of public housing, completing repairs, delivering food, or shipping goods must take into account both stochastic supply and demand.








To capture the dynamic nature of such settings, we develop a model in which a homogeneous good or service arrives according to a Poisson process with rate  $\mu > 0$, while potential buyers arrive according to an independent Poisson process with rate  $\lambda > 0$. Each buyer privately draws a valuation $v\in [0,1]$ from a common distribution function $F$. This valuation is the buyer's private information, unknown to other buyers or the seller. The seller has the option to maintain a queue for buyers, incurring a cost of $c>0$ per unit time for each buyer waiting in the queue. We consider both the case without storage, where an arriving item perishes unless immediately allocated, and the case with storage, where items can be held in inventory at a cost $d>0$ per unit time per item. For the latter case, the only restriction is that the allocation cannot be delayed, which implies that either the buyer queue or the item inventory must be empty; this restriction is without loss when the market is sufficiently dense in the sense that $\l,\m$ are sufficiently large, or either $c$ or $d$ is sufficiently small.\footnote{ Many gig platforms have both service providers and customers simultaneously waiting in queues.  While we abstract away from this possibility in our analysis, doing so is not too restrictive in our setting, as shown in the large market context. This is because we are considering homogeneous goods or job types.}
The seller can collect payment from a buyer at any point after the buyer's arrival.


The model encompasses distinct real-world scenarios. Service centers, like cloud computing or maintenance/repair facilities, are an example where service is perishable ($ d=\infty$). Here, the arrival of a service signifies its ``completion'', either utilized by a waiting customer or wasted if none are present. More broadly, platforms that dynamically match supplies or suppliers with customers fit our general framework with  $c,d<\infty$, where both supplies and buyers can be stored.\footnote{This interpretation ignores the waiting cost incurred by the customer (or the service provider) while service is being provided. To incorporate these costs, one can simply reinterpret $v$ as the expected surplus net of these waiting costs.}   Examples range from platforms for on-demand service, freelance, professional service, e-commerce, energy, water resources, freight ports, airports, ride-sharing, and supply-chain systems to organ donation networks and online advertising platforms.


The seller commits to a {\bf Markovian policy} which governs the entry and exit of buyers, inventory of goods, and their allocation, all contingent on the current state---that is, the number of buyers in the queue, their reported valuations, and the inventory level.  Each such policy induces a Markov chain over the states. Our goal is to find the policy that maximizes the seller's expected revenue under the Markov chain's stationary distribution.\footnote{It is possible to relax the restriction to Markovian policies and stationary distributions (see \Cref{rem:general}).}



The optimal policy works as follows. When a unit of the good arrives:
\begin{itemize}
    \item If buyers waiting, the good is allocated to the buyer with the highest valuation.
    \item  If no buyers are in the queue, the good is stored up to a maximum inventory level. Once this limit is reached, any additional goods are discarded.
\end{itemize}
When a new buyer arrives:
\begin{itemize}
    \item If there are $\ell>0$ goods in inventory, the buyer is assigned a good if his valuation exceeds a threshold $\hat{v}_{-\ell}$. Otherwise,  he is turned away.
    \item If the inventory is empty ($\ell = 0$), the buyer may join the queue. Suppose there were $k-1\geq 0$ existing buyers in the queue. The queue size then grows to $k$ if all $k$ buyers (including the new one) have valuations above a threshold $\hat{v}_k$. If not, the buyer with the lowest valuation is removed from the queue, and the queue size remains at $k - 1$. 
\end{itemize}

The threshold value $\hat{v}_j$ represents the seller's opportunity cost.  When $j$ is negative, it signifies the cost of immediately allocating a good to a new buyer. When $j$ is positive, it represents the cost of admitting a buyer into the queue for potential future allocation. The key feature is that $\hat{v}_j$ is increasing in $j$.
As the inventory shrinks or the queue grows longer, the seller becomes more selective, requiring higher buyer valuations to justify either immediate allocation or entry for potential future sales.  

The optimal policy strategically manages both buyer queues and item inventory to shift competitive pressure across time, responding to current and future market conditions. When demand is high, the policy ``saves" competitive pressure by queueing buyers, so that future goods will be contested by more high-value buyers. Conversely, when items arrive with buyers waiting, the policy ``borrows" competition from the future to discipline current buyers. This strategic use of future competition is reflected in our dominant-strategy implementation, which combines pricing and auction mechanisms as follows: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item When a buyer arrives and there are $\ell$ goods in inventory, the seller charges a {\bf monopoly price} of $\hat v_{-\ell}$.

    \item When a buyer arrives and there are $k-1\ge 0$ buyers already in the queue, a {\bf survival auction} is held, where buyers compete to stay in the queue. If the lowest-valued buyer has valuation below $\hat v_k$, he is removed. Otherwise, all buyers survive, and their personalized reserve prices rise to $\hat v_k$. The auction dynamically adjusts the queue size and ensures that only buyers with sufficiently high valuations remain.

    \item When a good arrives and buyers are waiting, an {\bf assignment auction} allocates the good. Each buyer in the queue submits a bid no less than his personalized reserve price, which reflects his previous participation in survival auctions. The highest bidder wins the good and pays his {\it cutoff price}, defined as the lowest bid he could have made and still obtained a good, possibly based on the events unfolding after the auction. 
\end{itemize}


The dominant strategy implementation makes clear how our optimal policy shifts competitive pressure across time to discipline winning buyers. The policy encodes and leverages past market competition through reserve prices, which are raised whenever a new buyer enters the queue. Likewise, future competition influences the cutoff price paid by a winning buyer. If the assignment of a good to the winner is followed by a spell of weak competition (e.g.,  arrival of many goods), the winner is charged a relatively low price.  If instead the assignment is followed by a period of strong competition (e.g., arrival of many high-value buyers), the winner's price is adjusted upward to reflect the increased competition.  To implement this feature in dominant strategies, the winner's payment must be delayed to reflect the unfolding market condition, or else buyers' incentives will be subject to their beliefs about that condition. Nevertheless, we show that such payment delays can be avoided by weakening the solution concept to periodic ex-post implementation \citep{bergemann2010dynamic}.



Our optimal policy exhibits attractive large-market properties.   As the market ``thickens''---that is, as $\l$ and $\m$ grow large at a constant ratio $\rho:=\l/\m$---or as either the queueing cost $c$ or storage cost $d$ vanishes, the optimal policy converges to a familiar multiunit auction: {\it a uniform-price auction with a standard monopoly reserve price.}  Specifically, imagine a static market with a unit mass of goods and a mass $\rho$ of buyers with valuations drawn independently from $F$. Then, it would be optimal to set a uniform price equal to the highest losing bidder or the monopoly reserve price, whichever is higher.  We show that our policy converges to this benchmark in terms of the seller's expected per-unit revenue.

While we focus primarily on revenue maximization, our framework can easily be extended to study welfare maximization. The welfare-maximizing policy exhibits qualitatively the same structure as the revenue-maximizing policy---buyers must meet higher thresholds to enter longer queues.  
However, lacking the incentive for monopoly exclusion, the welfare-maximizing seller is more willing to admit buyers into the queue when the queue is relatively short, to reduce the risk of goods arriving when there are no buyers (buyer stockouts). 
At the same time, the revenue-maximizing seller is more willing to admit buyers into a long queue, since she is more keen on selling to high-value buyers who command lower information rent, and because 
the increased permissiveness under shorter queues already reduces the risk of goods arriving without buyers.
Consequently, compared to revenue maximization, welfare-maximizing thresholds are lower for short queues and higher for long queues.

 We adopt the steady-state mechanism design approach, imposing stationarity of the state distribution as part of the solution concept.  As we argue below (Remark ..), this restriction is without loss.
This allows us to recast a fully dynamic mechanism design problem into a static linear program. Compared to recent studies that consider discrete states \citep{baccara2020optimal,che2021optimal,madsen2025collective},\footnote{\cite{baccara2020optimal} and \cite{madsen2025collective} consider two states, whereas \cite{che2021optimal} considers a countable number of states. 
} our problem involves a richer, infinite-dimensional state space that consists of all possible value profiles of buyers in the queue. 
We show that the problem can be tractably analyzed by exploiting suitably-defined marginals of the stationary distributions. This technique may potentially be applicable beyond our specific context.


















\paragraph*{Related Literature}   The present paper marries the classic mechanism design literature, exemplified by Myerson (1981), with the rational queueing theory, initiated by Naor (1969). While classic auction design typically assumes a static setting, our model captures the dynamic stochastic nature of the modern digital marketplace. In essence, we tackle a Myersonian auction design problem in the dynamic framework of queueing systems. Adapting the mechanism design analysis to the dynamic environment is not trivial.\footnote{In particular, the problem is not reduced to a simple extension of the classical model to allow for a stochastic number of buyers (e.g., \cite{mcafee1987auctions}).} The chosen mechanism must be implementable by a Markovian policy, ensuring that the interim allocation promised to a buyer is compatible with allocations promised to past and future buyers and the stochastically arriving supply. From the vantage point of the rational queueing theory, the current paper brings a full-fledged mechanism design perspective, allowing for the optimization of all aspects of system design, including the entry and exit of buyers and goods and the allocation decisions themselves.






Within the rational queueing theory, the closest antecedent to our paper is \cite{che2021optimal}, which also applies a comprehensive mechanism design perspective to the optimal queue design, allowing for full designer control of agents' entry and exit, service priority allocation, and information provision.\footnote{Several papers explore mechanism design with a more limited scope.  Examples include \cite{naor1969regulation} and \cite{hassin1985}, and \cite{leshnoAER}, which study alternative queueing rules under complete information.  \cite{lingenbrink2019optimal} and \cite{Anunrojwong2020} study optimal information policy under first-come-first-served (FCFS) as a queueing rule. }  
However, there are two key distinctions. First, we consider heterogeneous and private agent preferences, while \cite{che2021optimal} assumes homogeneous preferences. This significantly impacts the optimal policy regarding which agents join, remain in, and are served by the queue, forming a core theme of our paper. Second, we adopt a transferable utility setup, contrasting with their non-transferable utility model. This allows the designer to internalize waiting costs through ments, making the specific queueing rule irrelevant.  Nevertheless, the waiting cost drives a key departure from classic mechanisms; for instance, the optimal reserve price now varies with queue length.
Methodologically, by extending the steady-state mechanism design approach of \cite{che2021optimal}, we are able to handle a rich, infinite-dimensional state space.







The current paper connects with several queueing papers that explore the optimal screening of agents with heterogeneous preferences.  \cite{mendelson1990optimal}, \cite{afeche2013incentive}, \cite{afeche2016optimal}, and \cite{kittsteiner2005priority}  all consider the mechanism design problem with agents who have transferable utilities and heterogeneous preferences concerning the value of service, the cost of delay/waiting, and/or the required service time. The crucial distinction from this literature is that these papers study the mechanisms that are ``static'' in the sense that the allocation/screening does not depend on the current state---the queue size and its composition.  They also do not allow agents to depart from the queue.  By contrast, the mechanism we consider is fully dynamic, in which the allocation/screening decision depends on the profile of types of agents in the queue.

This paper also relates to the literature on dynamic revenue management \citep{gallego1994optimal,gershkov2009dynamic,gershkov2009learning,pai2013optimal,board2016revenue,gershkov2018revenue}. These papers study the allocation of a fixed supply of goods to buyers who arrive stochastically over time, often with a deadline (as with the airline ticket sales). The fact that only one side of the market faces stochastic arrival makes the problem nonstationary, leading to a quite distinct analysis from ours.\footnote{More broadly, the literature has studied optimal dynamic mechanisms when agents receive private information across multiple periods \citep{bergemann2010dynamic, athey_efficient_2013,pavan_dynamic_2014}.}











Finally, we contribute to the growing literature on dynamic matching, as seen in works by \cite{akbarpour2017thickness}, %
\citet{akbarpour2020unpaired}, \cite{baccara2020optimal}, \cite%
{leshno2019dynamic}, \cite{doval2018efficiency}, and \cite{ashlagi2023matching}.  
While these papers typically study a non-transferable utility (NTU) environment, we consider fully transferable utilities (TU) and allow for a broad spectrum of preference heterogeneity, akin to Myerson's classic work. (See \cite{fershtman2022matching} for a dynamic two-sided matching model with transferable utilities where agents rematch every period.)







\section{Setup}

\paragraph*{Primitives.} 

We consider a continuous time model in which a single seller (she) allocates goods to buyers (he).    At each instant $t\in (-\infty, \infty)$, the seller receives units of the good arriving at a Poisson rate of $\mu>0$. Buyers arrive at a Poisson rate of $\lambda>0$. 




Each buyer demands at most one unit of the good and values it at $v\in V:= [0,1]$, drawn independently for each buyer at the time of his arrival according to a CDF $F$ with a density $f$ that is strictly positive and absolutely continuous. We assume that the virtual value $J(v)\:=v-\frac{1-F(v)}{f(v)}$ is strictly increasing in $v$, and that $J(0)<0$.
Each buyer is privately informed of his value $v$.

When a buyer arrives but is not immediately allocated a good, the seller may ask him to join a queue. If a buyer declines, he leaves irreversibly and collects zero payoff. If a buyer agrees to join, he stays in the queue until he irreversibly exits the queue, which occurs when the buyer is allocated a good, when the buyer leaves voluntarily, or when the seller removes the buyer. A buyer incurs a waiting cost of $c\geq 0$ per unit of time he stays in the queue. The buyers do not observe the state of the queue, including the total number of buyers waiting or their valuations.



If a buyer with value $v$ spends time $t$ in the queue, makes a monetary payment of $y$, and receives the good, his payoff is $v-y-ct$. If he spends time $t$ in the queue and pays $y$ but does not receive the good, his payoff is $-y-ct$.

When a good arrives but is not immediately allocated to a buyer, the seller may discard the good or store it in inventory. For each good in inventory, the seller incurs a cost of $d>0$ per unit time. 



By assuming that a buyer stops incurring the waiting cost once he leaves the queue even if he has not received the good, we are considering environments in which buyers have outside options.  
An outside option could be simply to avoid the physical suffering of standing in line by foregoing the service. 
Also, customers often have alternative, perhaps less valuable, options they can substitute. For example, a buyer of cloud computing can look for other services, a customer of a gig platform can switch to a different one, ships queueing to unload their freights at a port can head out to a different nearby port, and a kidney patient may opt for a kidney transplant from a blood-type incompatible donor instead of joining a queue for an exchange with a cross-compatible patient-donor pair.

Our model combines features of the standard auction and queueing models. Our model resembles the Myerson framework with quasilinear preferences; the main difference is that both the goods and the buyers arrive stochastically over time, and waiting costs are incurred if buyers or units are not immediately allocated. When these costs vanish, our model will reduce to an auction with a stochastic number of buyers or goods, depending on the value of $\l/\m$. 
Our model also nests the standard $M/M/1$ queueing model, as studied in \cite{naor1969regulation}, which often considers buyers with homogeneous preferences. Such a model will be obtained if $c>0$, $d\to \infty$, and $V$ is a singleton.  In particular, our model can accommodate several distinct realistic settings:
\begin{itemize}
    \item {\bf Service scenario:}  Service centers, broadly defined, are providers of any perishable goods or services.  Cloud computing, repair and maintenance services, and customer services all fall into this category.
 This is a special case of our model where $d=\infty$ so that only buyers can wait in a queue, and items cannot be stored.  The arrival of goods is interpreted as ``service completion''
   only in case buyers are waiting;  if the ``service completion'' arrives when there are no buyers, then it is wasted.

   \item {\bf Service scenario with no preemption:}  The above scenario permits preemption, meaning a customer  in the middle of service can be replaced by another customer whenever it is desirable for the seller.  Given the memorylessness of the Markov process, such an interruption entails no inefficiencies and thus may occur in optimal policy. Preemption is not uncommon in practice.\footnote{A well-known example is the AWS services that subject customers opting for a discount service contract to preemption on short notice.}  Yet, there are situations where service interruption may be undesirable. Our model can accommodate this scenario because it is isomorphic to a scenario in which preemption is allowed, and at most one good can be stored.\footnote{Without preemption, a customer who begins service is eventually served with certainty. We may therefore interpret the starting of service as assignment, interpret the server being idle as having one good stored, and interpret the server being busy as having no goods stored. The additional waiting cost which the customer continues to incur while he is being served (i.e. when the server is busy) can be captured by replacing the value of assignment with the value net of this additional waiting cost.} 
   \item {\bf Goods and dynamic matching scenario:}  Retail platforms, dating apps, gig platforms, agencies for matching human organs,  public housing, adoptions, and foster care, 
   mediate or match agents or resources on two-sided markets.  In these environments, entities on both sides can be held in queues if immediate matches are impossible or undesirable. While some of these examples fall outside the scope of the current paper (particularly due to the transferability assumption and the no allocation delay assumption made below), the dynamic structure of these environments is accommodated with our general model with $c,d<\infty$. 
\end{itemize}


Our analysis proceeds in two steps.  We first consider the service scenario with $d=\infty$.   \cref{sec:storability} then investigates the general model with $d<\infty$.  The reason for this is twofold.  First, the case of $d=\infty$ constitutes a clear and significant merging of two frameworks, mechanism design a la \cite{myerson1981} and queueing a la \cite{naor1969regulation}.  Hence, the novel elements both in the modeling and analysis will be most transparent without further generalization involved. Second, with the number of features limited to its conceptual minimum, the exposition will be simpler. 







\paragraph*{Seller's Dynamic Policy for $d=\infty$.}\label{subsec:seller's-dynamic-policy}
\def\bv{\mathbf v}
\def\bV{\mathbf{V}}

That $d=\infty$ means that the item is either not storable or that the arrival of an item represents the completion of a service; namely, when the buyer queue is not empty, some buyer may be served at a Poisson rate no greater than $\mu$. 
The seller may invite buyers into a queue and keep them waiting, say, until items arrive, in which case she may allocate items to the buyers in the queue.  Hence, the seller's policy concerns which set of buyers the seller may keep in her queue and which buyer to allocate an arriving item to.  


By the revelation principle, it is without loss to focus on policies in which each buyer, upon entry, is incentivized to reveal his value.
The seller specifies a policy that depends on the payoff-relevant state, which consists of {\it the number of buyers in the queue, and the profile of their (reported) values}. Let $v_k$ denote the value of the buyer in the queue whose value is the $k$-th highest among all buyers in the queue, where we define $v_k:=0$ if there are fewer than $k$ buyers in the queue.  In particular, if the queue is empty, then $v_1=0$.  
Formally, a \textbf{state} is a tuple $\mathbf{v}\in \mathbf{V}$, where $\mathbf{V}$ is the set of $ (v_k)_{k\in\N} \in [0,1]^\N$ such that (i) $v_k\ge v_{k+1}$ for all $k\in \N$, and (ii) $v_k=0$ for all $k> k'$, for some $k'\in \N$.\footnote{When $v_k=0$, we are unable to distinguish a queue where some buyers have value 0, and another queue which consists of those buyers in the first queue with nonzero values. However, let us simply assume that the seller always turns away a buyer with value 0, as assigning a good to such a buyer will never benefit the seller. Subsequently, we use $v_k=0$ to mean that there are fewer than $k$ buyers in the queue. \label{v_k:=0}} 

The payoff-relevance of the state $\mathbf{v}$ is clear.  What may be less clear is its sufficiency; namely,  the policy need not depend on any information beyond this state.  First, we will focus on the steady state of the system, which makes calendar time irrelevant.  Second, by focusing on the (descendingly)-ordered list of values, we ignore the identities of the buyers beyond their values; evidently, this restriction entails no loss.\footnote{If an optimal mechanism discriminates buyers based on their identities, the ``same'' mechanism based on the permuted identities would also be optimal. Then, randomizing uniformly across all permutations of identities will also be optimal, and the resulting random mechanism will be anonymous.}  Third, the amount of time each buyer has already spent in the queue is not payoff-relevant because the service time is memoryless. While making the policy depend on the time spent in the queue could potentially affect the buyers' incentives, the transferability of utilities together with the homogeneity of the waiting cost, means that the seller can simply internalize the buyers' waiting costs by reimbursing them. Therefore, it is without loss to focus on  {Markovian polices}; see \Cref{rem:general} for details.


A \textbf{Markovian policy} depends only on the state $\mathbf{v}$ as it evolves, and consists of the following components:
\vspace{1em}






    $\bullet$ \textbf{Assignment/Service Priority:}   For each state  $\mathbf{v} \in [0,1]^\N$,   the seller decides whether to assign an incoming item and, if so, which buyer in the queue receives the item.  It is convenient to express this decision using the ex ante assignment rate. For each state  $\mathbf{v}$,  the assignment specifies functions $\exanteassign: \bV\to [0,\mu]^{\mathbb{N}}$, where  $\exanteassign_k(\bv)$, $\forall k\in \mathbb{N}$, is the ex ante rate of assigning the good to the buyer with the $k$-th highest value.  Feasibility requires that $\sum_k \exanteassign_k(\bv)\le \mu$. This formalization is particularly useful for the service scenario interpretation, according to which, whenever the queue is nonempty, one or more buyers can be served at a total rate of up to $\mu$. Note we are not assuming work conservation; the seller may withhold or delay service, in which case we have $\sum_k \exanteassign_k(\bv)< \mu$.\footnote{Since our assignment policy depends only on $\mathbf{v}$ and not on the time spent by each buyer on the queue, we are not including standard queueing rules such as first-come first-served.  However, as already noted, there is no loss in ruling out these policies. See also \cref{rem:general} below.
    }


$\bullet$ \textbf{Queue management:}  
    For each state $\mathbf{v}$, the seller determines whether to admit a newly arriving buyer into the queue and/or remove buyers from the queue. Formally, for each $\bv\in \bV$ and any measurable set $V'\subset V$ of types, the entry rule specifies a nonnegative ex ante rate $\phi(V';\bv) \le \lambda \int_{V'}\ddd F$ at which a buyer with type in $V'$ enters the queue.  The upper bound $\l\int_{V'}\ddd F$ corresponds to the unrestricted entry for the types $V'$, and the zero rate corresponds to a fully blocked entry. Meanwhile, for each $\bv\in \bV$, the exit rule specifies a function $\psi: \bV \to \mathbb{R}_+^{\mathbb{N}}$, where $\psi_k(\bv)$  is the ex ante rate at  which the buyer with the $k-$th highest value exits the queue for each $k\in \mathbb{N}$. This rule allows for an arbitrary set $N'\subset \mathbb{N}$ of buyers to be removed when a buyer arrives (in which case $\psi_k(\bv)=\l, \forall k\in N'$) or when an item arrives (in which case $\psi_k(\bv)=\m, \forall k\in N'$), or at some Poisson rate, independently of these events.  
    Consistency with the assignment rule requires that $\psi_k(\cdot)\ge \exanteassign_k(\cdot)$ for each $k\in \mathbb{N}$. 








$\bullet$  \textbf{Payment:} As is well known from the mechanism design literature, the risk neutrality of buyers means that how the payment is structured across times and across various events (e.g., assignment of goods) is immaterial. For ease of exposition and conformance to the mechanism design literature, we initially assume that the incoming buyer is charged an expected payment  $t(v; \bv)$, given his report $v$ and the state $\bv$.\footnote{How this expected payment is spread over time and across future contingencies remains unspecified; as this is not necessary for our analysis. } A more realistic and plausible payment rule will be introduced later when we discuss the dominant strategy implementation of our mechanism.


$\bullet$  \textbf{Information:}  Since the seller's policy depends only on the state $\bv$, it is a sufficient statistic for buyers' payoffs.  Hence, the information rule may specify any information about the state to existing and newly arriving buyers.  A buyer in the queue then updates his belief about the state based on that information as well as the time he has spent in the queue.  Formally, the information rule $\iota=(\iota^t)$ is simply the distribution of beliefs one has on the current state $\bv$ after each time $t$ is spent on the queue.   Throughout, we focus on the optimal information policy, which is to disclose no information beyond the recommendation made to each buyer at each instant.  Unlike \cite{che2021optimal}, buyers have heterogeneous preferences, and different types are treated differently depending on the state.  Hence,  the recommendation itself may reveal rich  information about the state of the queue.
Furthermore, the optimal policy implemented via dominant strategies permits more information than the recommendation to be disclosed to the buyers.\footnote{By contrast, the optimal information in \cite{che2021optimal} involves a relatively simple  recommendation, so the agents learn little about the queue length (the queue state in that model). In particular, an agent learns nothing about the queue length once he joins the queue.  In contrast, as will become clear, our optimal mechanism can be implemented with the buyers fully informed of the queue length all the time.}   


$\bullet$ \textbf{Incentives:}   The seller can prevent a buyer from joining the queue or force a buyer to leave the queue.  This is without loss because the seller can implement this by promising never to allocate a good or charging a high enough payment for the buyer.  Meanwhile, the seller cannot compel a buyer to join or stay in the queue against his wishes; such behavior must be incentivized through transfers or allocation.  













\paragraph*{Steady State Analysis.}  Given the primitive arrival and service processes, the feasible assignment and queue management rule $M=(\exanteassign,\phi, \psi)$ induces a Markov chain on $\bv$. We focus on the stationary distribution $\pi$ of this Markov chain. Formally, we restrict attention to Markovian policies whose associated Markov chain admits a stationary distribution.
This restriction is not particularly demanding.
For instance, it is reasonable that under a Markov policy chosen by the seller, the state with an empty queue, denoted by $\mathbf 0$, should be positive recurrent, meaning the state  $\mathbf 0$ has a finite mean return time.\footnote{An empty queue $\mathbf 0$ may fail to be positive recurrent because either the seller maintains an infinitely large queue or she refuses to serve waiting buyers, neither of which is consistent with revenue maximization.  An infinite queue incurs prohibitively high waiting costs, in which case either buyers would become unwilling to participate, or the seller would obtain a negative revenue after she compensates the buyers.  The seller refusing to serve a buyer regardless of the type appears inconsistent with the revenue objective. 
Regardless, if the queue is not infinite, the process may still admit a stationary distribution, in which case our framework still applies.}
Then, the process is regenerative and thus admits a unique limit distribution (\cite{asmussen2003applied} p. 170, Theorem 1.2), and this distribution must be stationary (\cite{THORISSON1992237}).










\paragraph*{Seller's Problem.}


Let $\mathcal{M}$ be the set of all feasible assignment and queue management rules $M=(\exanteassign,\phi,\psi)$ that admit a stationary distribution, and for each $M\in \mathcal M$, let $\Pi_M$ denote the set of stationary distributions induced by $M$.  We say that the Markovian policy $(M,t,\iota)$ is {\bf incentive-compatible at} $\pi \in \Pi_M$, {\it if there is an equilibrium in which each buyer, upon arrival, reports his value truthfully to the seller and acts according to $M$ from then on when they receive information according to $\iota$ starting with the prior belief on $\bv$ given by the stationary distribution $\pi$}.


We can describe the seller's problem as: 
 $$\sup_{M, t, \iota, \pi }  \int_{\bv}\int_0^1 \l t(v; \bv) f(v)\ddd v\,\pi(\dd\bv) \leqno{[\mathcal P_0]} $$   
subject to  
\begin{align*}
& M\in \mathcal M, \pi\in \Pi_M, \mbox{ and } (M,t, \iota) \text{ is incentive compatible at }\pi.
\end{align*}

Ultimately, our object of interest is a Markov policy that solves $[\mathcal P_0]$. Rather than directly solving $[\mathcal P_0]$, which may be difficult, we take an indirect approach. We first introduce and solve a relaxed program (\cref{sec:relaxed-program}). We then show that the optimal solution to the relaxed program can be implemented by an incentive compatible Markov policy and thus constitutes an optimal solution to $[\mathcal P_0]$ (\cref{sec:optimal-mechanism}).

\begin{rem} \label{rem:general}  Although we restrict attention to Markov policies, this restriction is without loss.  To see this, consider a more general {\bf regenerative policy}, which conditions all decisions on the entire (anonymous) history within each cycle bookended by empty queues ($\mathbf 0$)---the arrival time of each agent and his reported type and his departure time with or without an item, the arrival and departure of each item, its assignment, since the last time the queue is empty, until the queue goes empty again. Since events are independent across cycles and the seller's revenue is additively separable across cycles, such a policy is without loss. 

Such a regenerative policy induces a stochastic process whose projection into our state space $\bV$ constitutes a process that is regenerative, though not necessarily Markov. 
Since the process is nonlattice, as long as it is positive recurrent, it admits a unique stationary distribution as well as the long-run occupancy measure. 
Suppose we redefine $(M, t, \iota)$ to be a function of within-cycle history $h\in \mathcal H$, redefine $\Pi_M$ to be the set of occupancy measures induced by $M$, and redefine $t(v;\bv):=\E_{h\in \mathcal H}[\tau(v; h)|\bv]$, where $\tau(v; h)$ is the expected payment made by a buyer who arrives at history $h\in \mathcal H$ and reports $v$.  Then, one can simply recast  $[\mathcal P_0]$ as maximizing the long-run time-average of expected revenue subject to the incentive constraint being satisfied for all histories.  

The program $[\mathcal P_0]$ is now more general and in principle could have a higher value. Nevertheless, our program $[\mathcal P]$ below can be seen as its relaxation.  Since our later result shows that a Markovian policy attains the value of $[\mathcal P]$, the Markov restriction is without loss.  Intuitively, a Markovian policy is optimal because our primitive processes governing the arrival of buyers and items are memoryless.  
\end{rem}















\section{Feasibility Constraints and Relaxed Program}\label{sec:relaxed-program}



To write down our relaxed program, we first introduce a direct revelation mechanism (DRM)---an interim allocation, transfer, and waiting time for each arriving buyer---that is induced by a Markov policy at a steady state equilibrium. We require that a DRM satisfies interim incentive compatibility and participation constraint for buyers (\cref{subsec:drm}). Next, we impose some conditions that are necessary for a DRM to be consistent with a Markov policy and a steady state induced by the policy (\cref{subsec:rf}). We also impose feasibility conditions for the steady state distribution (\cref{subsec:balance}). Our relaxed program maximizes the seller's expected revenue subject to these constraints (\cref{subsec:relaxed}).





\subsection{Direct Revelation Mechanism} \label{subsec:drm}

For each incoming buyer, a Markov policy $(M,t,\iota)$ induces in a steady state equilibrium a \textbf{direct revelation mechanism (DRM)}, which is a triple of measurable functions $(X,T,W): V\to [0,1]\times \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}_+$, where
\begin{itemize}\itemsep.2em
    \item $X(v)$ is the {\it interim allocation probability} with which the buyers obtains a good,
    \item $T(v)$ is the {\it interim expected payment} that the buyer makes to the seller,
\item $W(v)$ is the {\it interim expected waiting time},
\end{itemize}
when the buyer reports $v$.\footnote{To connect with formulation in $[\mathcal P_0]$, we have $T(v)=\int t(v;\bv)\pi(d\bv)$.}
Since a DRM $(X,T,W)$ captures an equilibrium outcome, it must satisfy the usual incentive constraints.
Formally, a DRM must satisfy the buyers' \textit{participation constraint}:
\begin{align}
   U(v):= vX(v) - T(v) - cW(v) \geq 0 \qquad \forall v\in V. \tag{$IR$}
\end{align}
and their \textit{incentive compatibility constraint}:
\begin{align}
    U(v) \geq vX(v') - T(v') - cW(v') \qquad \forall v,v'\in V. \tag{$IC$}
\end{align}
Clearly, $(IC)$ and $(IR)$ must be satisfied in any equilibrium.\footnote{Note that the RHS of IC is the payoff to a type-$v$ buyer who reports $v'$ and also follows the continuation strategy of the true type-$v'$ buyer, deciding to leave the queue if and only if a type-$v'$ would have done so. That is, $(IC)$ ignores double deviations and is necessary but not sufficient for buyers to report truthfully. 
} Initially, we only require that it is a Bayes-Nash equilibrium for all buyers to report truthfully. Adopting this weakest notion of implementation allows us to cast the widest net for identifying the optimal policy. We will later show that the optimal policy can be implemented in dominant strategies.

A DRM $(X,T, W)$ can be interpreted as the mechanism's promise of allocation, payment, and wait time, which is offered to each incoming buyer. A DRM offered to a buyer must be compatible with the supply of goods arriving stochastically, with the DRMs offered to other buyers also arriving stochastically, and, importantly, with the seller's policy governing the entry and exit of buyers and the assignment of goods. We turn to this issue next. 


\subsection{Reduced-Form Feasibility Constraint} \label{subsec:rf}

A DRM $(X,T,W)$ offered to an incoming buyer must be implementable by a feasible Markov policy at a steady state. 
In particular, this means that the interim allocation promised to a given buyer must be consistent with the promises made to other buyers and with the supply of goods.

To represent this constraint, we make use of two definitions. First, define the ``marginal'' stationary distribution $\{P_k(v)\}_{k}$, where, for each $k \in \N$,
$$P_k(v):=\text{Prob}\{v_k \leq v\}$$ denotes the CDF of the $k$-th highest value of the buyer in the queue. This captures the distributions of the highest value, the second-highest value, and so on of the buyers, when an outside observer takes a snapshot of the queue at a random time \citep{wolff1982poisson}.    While our formal analysis will characterize these CDFs, our exposition here will focus on
$$p_k(v):=P_{k+1}(v)-P_k(v), \, \forall k\in \mathbb{N},$$
which denotes the stationary probability that {\it exactly} $k$ buyers in the queue have valuations strictly above $v$. (We let $p_0(v)\:=P_1(v).$) Since we encode the absence of a buyer by the presence of a buyer with zero valuation, 
$p_k(0)=P_{k+1}(0)-P_k(0)$ denotes the probability that there are exactly $k$ buyers in the steady state queue, where $P_0(0):=0$. 

For the second definition, suppose the state $\bv$ is such that there are exactly $k$ buyers with values weakly above $v$, and a good arrives. Conditional on this event, define $y_k(v)$ to be the probability that the arriving good is allocated to one of the $k$ buyers with values above $v$.\footnote{Note that $y_k(v)$ depends not just the Markov policy, but also on the corresponding stationary distribution.}

The \textit{reduced-form feasibility constraint} is: 
\begin{align*}
   \lambda  \int^1_v X(s)f(s)ds
   \le \mu\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} y_k(v)p_{k}(v), \quad \forall v\in V. \tag{$RF$} \label{RF}
\end{align*}
$(RF)$ upper-bounds the interim allocation $X$ in terms of $(p_k)$ and $(y_k)$.
The LHS is the sum of allocation promises made to types above $v$ per unit of time. The RHS is the sum of allocations to the buyers with values above $v$ per unit time; note that an allocation occurs when a good arrives and there are buyers in the queue.  This condition is similar in spirit to \cite{border1991implementation} and \cite{che2013generalized} but embodies a temporal dimension.  

$(RF)$ plays a crucial role in connecting the interim allocation rule $X$ to a feasible Markov policy, enabling us to embed the Myerson mechanism design analysis into the dynamic stochastic queueing framework. 







\subsection{Balance Inequalities} \label{subsec:balance}

We now impose a set of balance conditions that must be satisfied by any stationary distribution $(p_k)$ induced from a feasible Markovian policy.
For $k\in \N$, let
$$S_k(v):=\{\bv : v_k\le v\}$$
denote the set of states in which the $k$-th highest buyer value $v_k$ is weakly below $v$. Equivalently, $S_k(v)$ is the set of states in which at most $k-1$ buyers have values strictly above $v$.
Note that the event $S_k(v)$ occurs with probability $P_k(v)=\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}p_j(v)$ at the steady state.  \Cref{fig:events-S_k(v)} depicts the projections of $S_1(v)$ and $S_2(v)$, for some arbitrary $v$, into the first two coordinates of the state space, $v_1$ and $v_2$.

