At a stationary distribution, the outflow from the set $S_k(v)$ must equal the inflow to $S_k(v)$. We capture a necessary condition for this requirement by the following \textit{balance inequality} for set $S_k(v)$:
\begin{align*}
     \lambda[1-F(v)]   p_{k-1}(v)    \ge   \mu y_{k}(v) p_k(v).  \tag{${B_k}$}  \label{Bk}   
\end{align*}
The LHS is an upper bound on the outflow from set $S_k(v)$. Suppose we are in the set of states in which there are exactly $k-1$ buyers with values strictly above $v$. This set is a subset of $S_k(v)$ and occurs with probability $p_{k-1}(v)$. At rate $\l[1-F(v)]$, a buyer with a value strictly above $v$ arrives. If this buyer joins the queue, and no buyer with a value above $v$ leaves the queue, the queue will now contain $k$ buyers with values strictly above $v$, and the state will no longer be in $S_k(v)$. This is an upper bound on outflow because, as the seller cannot create buyers out of thin air, the only way for the state to move out of $S_k(v)$ is if a buyer with a value above $v$ arrives. The actual outflow may be smaller because the seller may refuse entry to such a buyer or remove an existing buyer with a value above $v$.  

The RHS is a lower bound on inflow to $S_k(v)$. Consider the states where exactly $k$ buyers have values strictly above $v$. This set is a subset of $(S_k(v))^c$ and occurs with probability $p_k(v)$. An item arrives at rate $\mu$, and with probability $y_k(v)$, it is allocated to a buyer with a value above $v$.  If this occurs, the queue has exactly $k-1$ buyers with values strictly above $v$, so the state has entered $S_k(v)$. The actual inflow may be larger because the sellers can remove buyers at will. For example, the seller could remove a buyer or multiple buyers with values above $v$ from the queue with some probability whenever an item is assigned or when a new buyer arrives, or at some constant Poisson rate without any such event. 

By writing down a balance inequality (rather than an equality) using marginal stationary distributions, we are able to capture the necessary conditions for the steady state under all feasible Markov policies.

As we will see later, we have carefully chosen the definition of the sets $S_k(v)$ and the balance inequalities so that, at the solution to the relaxed program, for each $k$, \eqref{Bk} holds with equality as long as $v$ is above some threshold. 








\subsection{Relaxed Program} \label{subsec:relaxed}

We are now ready to formulate our relaxed program.   
Consider the problem:
\begin{align*}
    &\max_{p,y,X,T,W} \l \int_0^1 T(v)f(v)dv\\
\mbox{   subject to } & \quad
(IR),\, (IC),\, (RF),\mbox{ and } (B_k) \,\, \forall k. 
\end{align*}
One can see that this program is a relaxation of $[\mathcal P_0]$. 
First, any feasible assignment and queue management rule $M \in \ms M$ induces a Markov chain on $\bv$ and an associated stationary distribution $\pi\in \Pi_M$, which induces the marginals $(p_k)$ and the assignment rule $(y_k)$. Together with the payment policy $t$, they induce a DRM $(X,T,W)$. In sum,  the choice variables $(p,y,X,T,W)$ are induced by the choice variables in $[\mathcal P_0]$.
Second, the incentive compatibility of $(M,t,\iota)$ implies that $(X,T,W)$ satisfies $(IR)$ and $(IC)$. Third, for an interim allocation rule $X$ to be implemented by a feasible Markov policy in a steady state, it must satisfy $(RF)$. Last, the stationarity of the marginals $(p_k)$ requires the balance inequalities $(B_k)$ to be satisfied.



We next simplify the problem by appealing to the envelope theorem and Little's Law. Specifically, $(IC)$ and $(IR)$ implies that
\begin{align}
 \l \int_0^1 T(v)f(v)dv 
\le & \,  \l \int_0^1 \left[v X(v)-U(v)- c W(v)\right] f(v)dv\cr
= & \,  \l \int_0^1 \left[v X(v)-\int_0^v X(s)ds\right] f(v)dv - c \l \int_0^1 W(v) f(v)dv\cr 
= &\,   \l \int_0^1 J(v) X(v) f(v)dv - c \sum_{k=1}^\infty k  p_k(0), \label{eq:envelope}
\end{align}
where we recall that $J(v)\:= v - \frac{1-F(v)}{f(v)}$ is the  virtual value.  The last equality also uses Little's law, which asserts that ex-ante waiting time $\int_0^1 W(v) f(v)dv$ multiplied by the arrival rate $\l$ equals the average number of buyers in the queue, which contains $k$ buyers with probability $p_k(0)$ in the steady state.

Using (\ref{eq:envelope}), we can further relax the problem as follows:
\begin{equation*}
    \max_{p,y,X} \l \int_0^1 J(v)X(v)f(v)dv - c \sum_{k=1}^\infty kp_k(0) \leqno{[\mathcal P]}
\end{equation*}
$$
\mbox{ subject to }  \quad
  (RF) \mbox{ and } (B_k) \,\, \forall k. 
$$ 
The structure of program $[\mathcal P]$ provides an intuition for why we chose this particular form of balance inequalities and reduced-form feasibility constraints. The interim allocation $X(v)$ enters the objective function positively, and $X(v)$ is bounded above by the RHS of $(RF)$. In turn, the RHS of $(RF)$, which represents actual allocations per unit time, is bounded above by the balance inequalities $B_k$.


We are ready to present the optimal solution to $[\mathcal P]$.   

\begin{thm}\label{thm:relaxed-program}  There exists a triple $(P^*, y^*, X^*)$ that solves the relaxed program $[\mathcal P]$.  There exist thresholds $\hat v_1< \hat v_2 < ...< \hat v_{K^*}<1$, for some $K^*<\infty$, such that
\begin{itemize}
    \item  the CDF, $P^*_k(v)$, $k=1, ..., K^*$, is supported on $\{0\}\cup [\hat v_k, 1]$ and is continuous and strictly increasing in $v$ on $[\hat v_k, 1]$;\footnote{More precisely, for $k=1,...,K^*$, $P_k^*(0)$ is determined iteratively  by
$\frac{P^*_k(0)}{1+\rho\left(\hat{v}_k\right)+\cdots+\rho\left(\hat{v}_k\right)^{k-1}}=\frac{P^*_{k+1}(0)}{1+\rho\left(\hat{v}_k\right)+\cdots+\rho\left(\hat{v}_k\right)^k},
$
and  $P^*_k(0)=1$ for all $k \geq K^*+1$, where $\rho(v):=\l [1-F(v)]/\m$. 
 For $k=1, ..., K^*$, $P_k^*(v)$ is determined by
\begin{align*}
& P_1^*(v)=\frac{P^*_{i+1}(0)}{1+\rho(v)+\cdots+\rho(v)^i} \quad \forall v \in\left[\hat{v}_i, \hat{v}_{i+1}\right), \\
& P_k^*(v)=\left(1+\rho(v)+\cdots+\rho(v)^{k-1}\right) P_1^*(v) \quad \forall v \in\left[\hat{v}_i, \hat{v}_{i+1}\right), \forall k \in\{1, \ldots, i\}.
\end{align*}} 

    \item $y^*_k(v) = 1, \forall v\in[0,1], \forall k=1,\ldots,K^*$;

    \item $X^*(v) =  
        \dfrac{\m}{\l f(v)} {P_1^*}'(v)$ holds for almost all $v \in [0,1]$,
\end{itemize}
where the thresholds, $\hat v_1< \hat v_2 < ...< \hat v_{K^*}$, are determined iteratively: 
\begin{equation}
    J(\hat v_1)=\frac{c}{\m}, \mbox{ and }  \int_{\hat v_{k-1}}^{\hat v_k} \frac{P_1^*(v)}{P^*_{k}(0)} J'(v) d v=\frac{c}{\m}, \, \forall k=2, ...,K^*.  \label{eq51}
\end{equation} 
\end{thm}










\cref{sec:appendix-relaxed-program} proves the optimality of the solution $(P^*,y^*,X^*)$.  The intuition behind the structure of the solution will be provided more fully in the next section by the Markov policy implementing that solution.  Here, we observe a few qualitative features. 

The optimal assignment policy $y^*$ suggests non-wastefulness: whenever an item arrives with some buyers waiting, it is always assigned to some buyer, and in particular, it is always assigned to the highest buyer.
Next, the interim allocation $X^*$ is chosen so that \cref{RF} binds, given $y^*$. Its dependence on the density $P_1'^*(v)$ of the highest value in the steady state is consistent with $y^*$ always assigning an item to the highest buyer. 

Meanwhile, the supports of the stationary distribution $P_k^*$'s hint at the optimal queue management policy (see \cref{fig:marginal-CDF} for a case with $K^*=2$).  The seller accommodates $k$ buyers in the queue only if all have values above a certain cutoff $\hat v_k$, suggesting that the seller will prevent buyers from entering queues based on these cutoffs. This will be confirmed in the next section, where we present a Markov policy implementing $(P^*,y^*,X^*)$.
