\section{Optimal Policy} \label{sec:optimal-mechanism}

In this section, we present a Markov policy implementing the optimal solution to $[\mathcal P]$, which must then be the optimal solution to our original problem $[\mathcal P_0]$. Specifically, we will argue for the optimality of the
Markov policy $(M^*,t^*,\iota^*) = (\exanteassign^*,\phi^*,\psi^*,t^*,\iota^*)$ defined as follows:

\begin{itemize}\itemsep.5em
    \item \textbf{Assignment:}  Consistent with  \cref{thm:relaxed-program}, the policy assigns the item to a buyer with the highest valuation in the queue; recall the item is discarded when the queue is empty since $d=\infty$. 

    Formally, the ex ante assignment rate $\exanteassign^*: \bV\to [0,\mu]^{\mathbb{N}}$ is given by
    \begin{align*}
        \exanteassign^*_k(\bv) = \begin{cases}
           \m \caseif  k=1  \mbox{ and } v_1>0; \\
           0 & \text{otherwise.}
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    In the service context, the service is provided in the order of reported values, irrespective of the buyers' arrival orders. 
    \item \textbf{Buyer-queue management:} To accommodate $k$ buyers in the queue, \cref{thm:relaxed-program} requires all $k$ buyers to have values above $\hat v_k$. For each state $\bv$ and the associated number $\kappa(\bv):=\max \{k:v_k>0\}$ of buyers in the queue, an incoming buyer with reported value $v$ is {\it added} to the queue if and only if either  $v \geq \max\{\hat v_{\kappa(\bv)}, 
 v_{\kappa(\bv)}\}$ or $v \geq \hat v_{\kappa(\bv)+1}$. After the incoming buyer is added, the lowest-valued buyer is \textit{removed} from the queue if his value is below $\hat v_{\kappa(\bv)+1}$.


    Formally, the ex-ante entry rate is:
    \begin{align*}
        \phi^*(V';\bv) = \lambda \int_{V'} \I\{ v \geq \min\{v_{\kappa(\bv)}, \hat v_{\kappa(\bv)+1}\} \} F(\dd v),
    \end{align*}
    for each state $\bv$ and each measurable set $V'\subset [0,1]$.  Likewise, the  ex-ante exit rate $\psi^*: \bV \to \mathbb{R}_+^{\mathbb{N}}$ is given by
    \begin{align*}
        \psi^*_k(\bv) = \begin{cases}
            \mu \caseif  k=1\ne \kappa(\bv);  \\
            \mu + \l[1-F(v_1)] \caseif k=\kappa(\bv)=1 \mbox{ and } v_1<\hat v_2;\\
            \l[1-F(v_k)] \caseif k=\kappa(\bv)>1 \mbox{ and } v_k<\hat v_{k+1}; \\
            0 & \text{otherwise}.
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    \item \textbf{Payment:} A buyer who  reports $v$  is required to make a lump-sum payment
    $$v X^*(v)+\int^v_0 X^*(s)ds$$
    upon arrival 
    and is reimbursed (or receives a transfer equal to) the ex-post waiting cost upon exiting the queue. The reimbursement is possible since the seller observes the time each buyer spends in the queue and his unit-time waiting cost. 
    Observe that the payment function is designed to satisfy the standard envelope formula, allowing the Markov policy to be incentive-compatible and individually rational in the Bayesian sense. Given this payment function, the buyer has incentives to obey the Markov policy  since  the reimbursement policy makes a buyer indifferent to the time he spends waiting in the queue, 
   so that once a buyer pays the lump-sum transfer and joins the queue, he has no incentive to leave the queue voluntarily.  


    \item \textbf{Information:} No information is provided, other than the recommendation to join or stay in the queue as stipulated by $(\phi^*,\psi^*)$.
\end{itemize}

The result follows. 

\begin{thm}\label{thm:optimal-policy}
The Markov policy  $(M^*,t^*,\iota^*)$ induces a stationary distribution with marginals $P^*$, induces $(y^*, X^*)$, and is incentive compatible.
Therefore, $(M^*,t^*,\iota^*)$ is an optimal solution to $[\mathcal P_0]$.
\end{thm}

\Cref{thm:optimal-policy} says that the Markov policy $(M^*,t^*,\iota^*)$ implements the optimal solution $(P^*, y^*, X^*)$ to the relaxed program $[\mathcal P]$.   As a result, the seller's expected revenue under the policy is equal to the value of the relaxed program, which is clearly an upper bound on the seller's expected revenue that can be obtained under any feasible Markov policy. Thus, the policy is optimal.

It is instructive to explore several features of the optimal Markov policy.  First, as is standard, the efficiency of allocation follows from the regularity of the value distribution. Since the virtual value is increasing, assigning an arriving item to the buyer with the highest value (whenever the queue is nonempty) contributes to revenue maximization. 

Second, the optimal queue length exhibits a classic birth-and-death process, which at each instant goes up or down by one or stays the same; recall that this is not an assumption, since we allowed multiple buyers to be removed simultaneously. To illustrate why this is optimal, suppose there are currently $k\ge 1$ buyers in the queue.  The highest-value buyer is assigned an item when it arrives.  The lowest-value buyer is removed only when he has a value below $\hat v_{k+1}$ and is replaced by an incoming buyer with a higher value.  Clearly, this feature ensures that at the steady state, the marginal buyer's value is above the requisite threshold corresponding to the queue size. That is, consistent with the support of $P^*$, all buyers in the $k$-length queue have values above $\hat v_k$.

Third, it is worth noting that the value of the threshold depends only on the number of buyers in the queue, and not on their exact value profile. The intuition is that, under the optimal assignment rule, a buyer is assigned a good only after every other buyer in the queue with a higher value has been assigned, and this is true regardless of how much higher the other buyers' values are. Therefore, the expected time until a buyer is assigned depends on his own value and the number of buyers in the queue with higher values, but not on the values of these other buyers. Hence, whether a buyer's value justifies his waiting in the queue should also be determined solely by his own value and the number of other buyers with higher values.


Last, the thresholds $\hat v_k$ play a role similar to that of the reserve price in a static auction; buyers who do not meet the thresholds are never assigned the good, and this threat of exclusion lowers the buyers' information rents. However, unlike the reserve price, the thresholds increase in the number of buyers because buyers incur a waiting cost while waiting for a good to arrive. 






In showing that $M^*$ induces $X^*$, the key observation is that for a buyer who has value $v\in [\hat v_i,\hat v_{i+1})$ and joins the queue, his ranking in the queue in terms of value improves by 1 every time an item arrives, and it drops by 1 every time a higher-valued buyer arrives. Moreover, the buyer is assigned a good when his ranking reaches 0, and is removed from the queue when his ranking reaches $i+1$. Hence, his interim allocation probability can be obtained by employing the method analogous to solving the {\it gambler's ruin} problem, and can be shown to be equal to $X^*$.

One may also consider the joint stationary distribution of $\bv=(v_k)$ that is induced by the optimal policy $(M^*,t^*,\iota^*)$. \cref{fig:joint-CDF} depicts the stationary joint CDF of the value profile $(v_1,v_2)$ under the optimal policy, using parameters such that the maximum queue length under the optimal policy is $K^*=2$. A positive probability is placed on the point $(v_1,v_2)=(0,0)$, which represents the state in which the queue is empty. A positive probability is also placed on the interval $\{ (v_1,v_2) \mid \hat v_1 \leq v_1 \leq 1 \text{ and } v_2=0\}$, which is the set of states with exactly one buyer in the queue. The remaining probability is placed on the upper-right triangular region $\{ (v_1,v_2) \mid v_1 \geq v_2 \geq \hat v_2\}$, which is the set of states with two buyers in the queue.



