\paragraph*{Comparative Statics.}

The optimal threshold values respond in a natural way to changes in the parameters. 

\begin{prop}[Comparative Statics in $c,\l,\m$]\label{prop:thresholds-compstat}
    For $k=2,\ldots,K^*$, the threshold $\hat v_k$ is strictly increasing in $c$, strictly increasing in $\l$, and strictly decreasing in $\mu$.
    The first threshold $\hat v_1$ is strictly increasing in $c$, constant in $\l$, and strictly decreasing in $\mu$. 
\end{prop}





 Intuitively, when waiting becomes more costly (higher $c$) or items arrive less frequently (lower $\mu)$, the seller should become more selective in accepting buyers into the queue and, therefore, should raise the thresholds.
When buyers arrive more frequently (higher $\l$), a buyer who joins the queue is more quickly replaced with a new buyer who has a higher value. If a buyer joins an empty queue, he is served at a constant Poisson rate, so the prospect of quick replacement has no impact on whether the seller should accept a buyer into an empty queue. This is why $\hat v_1$ doesn't depend on $\l$.  However, if a buyer joins a nonempty queue, he is served at rate zero until all higher-valued buyers are served. Hence, as $\l$ increases, so that the buyer is more likely to be replaced before he makes it to the top of the queue, the seller is less willing to accept the buyer. This explains that $\hat v_k$ is increasing in $\l$.\footnote{To see this more formally, note that the time until the next arrival of an item follows the exponential distribution, which is memoryless. However, the time until the next $k\geq 2$ arrivals follows the Erlang distribution, which is not memoryless.}    These comparative statics are depicted in \cref{fig:comp-stat}. 

