\section{Storable Goods: $d<\infty$}\label{sec:storability}

We have so far considered the case in which goods cannot be stored ($d=\infty$). 
However, a common feature in dynamic two-sided markets, such as gig platforms, is that each side can wait for the other side to arrive.  This is captured by $d < \infty$, and we consider this scenario from this section onward. As will become clear, $d<\infty$ can be viewed as a generalization of $d=\infty$, since the optimal policy under $d<\infty$, when $d$ is large enough, reduces to the optimal policy under $d=\infty$.

Given finite storage costs, it is now possible for the seller to maintain a queue of goods (or inventory) and a queue of buyers.  For the analysis to be tractable, we require a policy restriction.  






\begin{assumption}[{\bf No allocation delay (NAD)}]  A good arriving with buyers available in the queue is either assigned immediately or discarded; a buyer arriving with goods available in inventory is either assigned immediately or turned away.\end{assumption}



NAD means that only one type of queue is active at any point: either the queue of buyers or the inventory of goods is empty. 
This provides analytical tractability because, though our state $\bv$ is infinite-dimensional,  the queue length remains effectively one-dimensional if we view states with goods stored as having a negative queue length.\footnote{That is, one can use $k\in \mathbb{N}$ to denote $k$ buyers in the queue, and use $k=-\ell$, for some $\ell\in \mathbb{N}$, to  denote $\ell$ goods in the inventory.} This structure, {\it double-ended queue (or dqueue)}, is often invoked in the Operations and the Computer Science literatures. Our version of dqueue is much less restrictive since we allow multiple buyers and multiple items to be removed, as long as the state does not jump between the buyer queue and the goods queue.  NAD is also reminiscent of {\it Work-Conservation} or {\it Non-Wastefulness}, often invoked in the queueing literature, but it is much weaker. In the service scenario context ($d=\infty$), NAD is satisfied vacuously.

While this assumption is not without loss, the economic case for holding buyers and goods simultaneously is not very strong (recall that the good is homogeneous and buyers have unit demands). Indeed, we show that NAD becomes without loss as the market grows thick in the sense that  $\l,\m\to \infty$, or $c$ or $d$ becomes small. This result is in line with the literature on dynamic matching, which often finds that matching agents greedily, rather than making them wait for better matches, is approximately optimal in large markets \citep{akbarpour2017thickness,ashlagi2023matching}. 



We now represent the state by a tuple $(\bv;\ell) \in \bV \times \Z_+$, where $\bv$ is the value profile of buyers and $\ell$ is the number of goods in inventory. NAD implies that we must have either $\bv=\mathbf{0}$ or $\ell=0$.
The seller's Markov policy is now a tuple $M=(x, \tilde x, \phi, \widetilde \phi, \psi, \widetilde \psi,t,\iota)$, where $(x,\phi,\psi,t,\iota)$ are the rules described in \cref{subsec:seller's-dynamic-policy}, and $(\tilde x,\widetilde \phi,\widetilde \psi)$ are additional rules defined as follows.


\vspace{1em}

$\bullet$ \textbf{Assignment of Arriving Buyers:} 
When goods are stored, the seller determines whether a newly arriving buyer is assigned a good. Formally, for any measurable set $V'\subset V$ of types and each state $(\mathbf{0};\ell)$ with $\ell\geq 1$, a buyer with type in $V'$ arrives and is assigned an item at an ex ante rate $ \tilde\exanteassign(V';\ell) \leq \l\int_{V'} \ddd F$.

$\bullet$ \textbf{Inventory management:}  When a good arrives, the seller can either store it in inventory or discard it (and/or other goods in the inventory). Formally, when the state is $(\mathbf{0}; \ell)$ with $\ell\geq 0$, a good is added to inventory at an ex-ante rate of $\widetilde \phi(\ell) \leq \mu$.  When the state is $(\mathbf{0}; \ell)$ with $\ell\geq 1$, a stored good is removed at an ex-ante rate of $\widetilde \psi (\ell)$. Consistency with the assignment rule requires that $ \widetilde \psi (\cdot) \geq \tilde \exanteassign(V;\cdot)$. 

\vspace{1em}

The steady state is now  $\pi = (P,q,y,z,X,T,W)$, where  $(P,y,X,T,W)$ are the objects described in \cref{subsec:seller's-dynamic-policy}, and $(q,z)$ are defined as follows:
\begin{itemize}\itemsep.2em
    \item  $q_\ell$ is the probability  that exactly the inventory is storing exactly $\ell$ goods.
    \item $z_\ell(v)$ is the probability that, conditional on the inventory storing exactly $\ell$ goods, an incoming buyer with value $v$ is assigned a good.
\end{itemize}

As before, we focus on an indirect approach based on solving a relaxed program. While the formulation of the direct revelation mechanism and the associated $(IC)$ and $(IR)$ remain unchanged, the reduced-form feasibility $(RF)$ and the balance conditions $(B)$ must be extended to accommodate inventory management.  

The reduced-form feasibility constraint now becomes   
\begin{align*}
   \lambda  \int^1_v X(s)f(s)ds
   \le \mu\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} y_k(v)p_k(v) + \l \sum_{l=1}^\infty q_\ell \int_v^1 f(s)z_\ell(s)ds, \, \forall v\in V. \tag{$\widetilde{RF}$} \label{RF-storage}
\end{align*}
The RHS of $(\widetilde{RF})$ represents allocations per unit time.  Note that allocation occurs not just when a good arrives with buyers waiting in the queue (the first term), but also when a buyer arrives with goods available (the second term).

For each $k\geq 1$ and $v\in[0,1]$, the set $S_k(v)$ now contains states in which there are no buyers and possibly some goods. The corresponding balance inequality, $B_k$, remains unchanged once we redefine $p_0(v)\:=P_1(v) - \sum_{\ell=1}^\infty q_{\ell}$  for all $v \in [0,1]$.\footnote{Recall that,  for the case of $d=\infty$, we had defined $p_0(v)\:=P_1(v)$.}
For $\ell \in \N$, let $S_{-\ell}$ denote the set of states in which there are at least $\ell$ goods in storage. 
Note that the probability of $S_{-\ell}$ is $\sum_{i=\ell}^\infty q_{i}$.
Under a stationary distribution, the outflow from $S_{-\ell}$ must be equal to the inflow to $S_{-\ell}$. This is captured by the balance inequality for set $S_{-\ell}$:
\begin{align*}
     \l  q_\ell \int_0^1 f(s)z_\ell(s)\dd s \leq \mu q_{\ell-1}, \tag{${B_{-\ell}}$}  \label{B-l}
\end{align*}
where we let $q_0 \:= P_1(0) - \sum_{\ell=1}^\infty q_{\ell}$ for convenience.\footnote{$q_0$ is the probability that there are no buyers in the queue nor any goods in inventory.}
The LHS is a lower bound on the outflow from set $S_{-\ell}$. Suppose there are exactly $\ell$ goods in storage. If a buyer arrives and is assigned a good, then there are now at most $\ell-1$ goods in storage, so the state is no longer in $S_{-\ell}$. The actual outflow may be larger because the seller may freely discard goods. The RHS is an upper bound on inflow to set $S_{-\ell}$. Suppose there are exactly $\ell-1$ goods stored. If a good arrives and is added to storage, there are now $\ell$ goods, and the state is now in $S_{-\ell}$. The actual inflow may be smaller because the arriving good may not be stored.\footnote{Whereas the balance inequality for $S_k(v)$ requires that outflow is no less than inflow, the balance inequality for $S_{-\ell}$ requires that outflow is no greater than inflow. 
}

The relaxed program is: 
\begin{align*}
    &\max_{p,q,y,z,X,T,W} \l \int_0^1 T(v)f(v)dv - d \sum_{\ell=1}^L \ell q_\ell\\
&\mbox{   subject to } \quad
 (IR),\, (IC),\,(\widetilde{RF}),\, (B_k)\,\,\forall k, \mbox{ and } (B_{-\ell})\,\,\forall \ell. 
\end{align*}
As before, by appealing to the envelope theorem and Little's Law, the relaxed program can be simplified to
\begin{equation*}
    \max_{p,q,y,z,X} \l \int_0^1 J(v)X(v)f(v)dv - c \sum_{k=1}^\infty k p_k(0)  - d \sum_{\ell=1}^\infty \ell q_\ell \leqno{[\widetilde{\ms P}]}
\end{equation*}
\begin{align*}
    \mbox{subject to}\quad (\widetilde{RF}),\, (B_k)\,\, \forall k, \, \mbox{and} \, (B_{-\ell})\,\, \forall \ell.
\end{align*}
The two terms on the RHS of $(\widetilde{RF})$ are bounded above by the balance inequalities $B_k$ and $B_{-\ell}$, respectively.

The following is an analogue of \cref{thm:relaxed-program}.

\begin{customthm}{$\mathbf{1'}$}\label{thm:relaxed-program-storable}
    There exists a tuple $(P^*,q^*,y^*,z^*,X^*)$ that solves the relaxed program $[\widetilde{\mathcal P}]$. There exist thresholds $\hat v_{-L^{*}} < \cdots < \hat v_{-2} < \hat v_{-1} <\hat v_1 < \hat v_2 < \cdots \hat v_{K^{*}}$, for some $K^*,L^* < \infty$, such that
    \begin{itemize}
        \item the CDF, $P^*_k(v)$, $k=1, ..., K^*$, is supported on $\{0\}\cup [\hat v_k, 1]$ and continuous and strictly increasing in $v$ on $(\hat v_k, 1)$;\footnote{More precisely, for $k=1,...,K^*$, $P_k^*(0)$ is determined iteratively  by
        $\frac{P^*_k(0) - Q^*_1}{1+\rho\left(\hat{v}_k\right)+\cdots+\rho\left(\hat{v}_k\right)^{k-1}}=\frac{P^*_{k+1}(0) - Q^*_1}{1+\rho\left(\hat{v}_k\right)+\cdots+\rho\left(\hat{v}_k\right)^k},$
        and  $P^*_k(0)=1$ for all $k \geq K^*+1$, where $\rho(v)\:=\l [1-F(v)]/\m$ and $Q^*_1 \:= \sum_{\ell=1}^{L^*} q^*_\ell$. 
        For $k=1, ..., K^*$, $P_k^*(v)$ is determined by
        \begin{align*}
        & P_1^*(v)=\frac{P^*_{i+1}(0) - Q^*_1}{1+\rho(v)+\cdots+\rho(v)^i} + Q^*_1 \quad \forall v \in\left[\hat{v}_i, \hat{v}_{i+1}\right), \\
        & P_k^*(v)=\left(1+\rho(v)+\cdots+\rho(v)^{k-1}\right) \left(P_1^*(v) - Q^*_1\right) + Q^*_1 \quad \forall v \in\left[\hat{v}_i, \hat{v}_{i+1}\right), \forall k \in\{1, \ldots, i\}.
                \end{align*}}
        \item $\m q^*_{\ell-1} = \l[1-F(\hat v_{-\ell})]q^*_\ell \qquad \forall \ell\leq L^*;$  
        \item $y^*_k(v) = 1, \forall v\in[0,1], \forall k=1,\ldots,K^*$;
        \item $z^*_\ell(v) = \mathbb{I}\{ v \geq \hat v_{-\ell} \}$;
        \item the interim allocation satisfies
        \begin{align*}
	        	X^*(v) = \begin{cases}
	        	\dfrac{\m}{\l f(v)} {P_1^*}'(v) + \sum_{\ell=1}^{L^*} q^*_\ell \caseif v \geq \hat v_1 \\
	        	\sum_{i=\ell}^{L^*} q^*_i \caseif v \in [\hat v_{-\ell},\hat v_{-\ell+1}), \ell=2,\ldots,L \\
	        	\sum_{i=1}^{L^*} q^*_i \caseif v \in [\hat v_{-1},\hat v_1) \\
	        	0 \caseif v \leq \hat v_{L^*}.
	        \end{cases}
            \end{align*}
    \end{itemize}
\end{customthm}
\cref{sec:appendix-relaxed-program} provides a more detailed characterization of the solution $(P^*,q^*,y^*,z^*,X^*)$. When $d$ is sufficiently large that storing a good is never optimal, we have $L^*=0$, and the solution here reduces to the solution $(P^*,y^*,X^*)$ that we obtained for the non-storable case in \cref{subsec:relaxed}.

Analogous to the case without storage, we present a Markov policy
$M^{*} = (x^*, \tilde x^*, \phi^*, \widetilde \phi^*, \psi^*, \widetilde \psi^*,t^{*},\iota^{*})$ that implements the solution to 
$[\widetilde{\ms P}]$. 
The thresholds are strictly increasing in queue length, i.e. $\hat v_i > \hat v_j $ for $i>j$. The positive thresholds are weakly higher compared to the case without storage; that is, if we let $\hat v_{k,d}$ denote the $k$-th threshold under a storage cost of $d$, we have $\hat v_{k,d} \geq \hat v_{k,\infty}$ for all $d<\infty$.
Buyer-queue management, payment, and information rules are unchanged except for the change in the threshold values. The assignment rule now also specifies how arriving buyers are assigned to stored goods:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Assignment:} Suppose the state is $(\mathbf{0};\ell)$ with $\ell\geq 1$, and a buyer with value $v$ arrives. The buyer is allocated a good if $v \geq \hat v_{-\ell}$ and is otherwise turned away. Formally, the ex ante assignment rate for arriving buyers with types in $V'$ is
    \begin{align*}
        \tilde \exanteassign^{*}(V';\ell) = \l \int_{V'} \mathbb{I}\{ v \geq \hat v_{-\ell} \} \ddd F.
    \end{align*}
\end{itemize}
The opportunity cost of assigning a stored good to an arriving buyer is the possibility that all stored goods are assigned in this manner, and then yet another buyer arrives with a higher value, who may eventually leave without being assigned. This possibility is less likely when there are more goods in storage. This is why $\hat v_{-\ell}$ is decreasing in $\ell$. Having more goods stored makes the seller become less selective in assigning goods.

Moreover, the Markov policy includes a new rule for managing the inventory of goods:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Inventory management:} Suppose the state is $(\mathbf{0};\ell)$, and a good arrives. The new good is stored if $\ell \leq L^*-1$ and is otherwise discarded. A stored good is never discarded until it is allocated to a buyer. Formally, when there are $\ell$ goods stored, a new good is added at an ex ante rate of
    \begin{align*}
        \widetilde \phi^{*}(\ell) = \begin{cases}
            \mu \caseif \ell \leq L^*-1\\
            0 \caseif \ell \geq L^*,
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    and a stored good is removed (i.e. allocated) at an ex ante rate of $\widetilde \psi^{*}(\ell) = \l (1-F(\hat v_{-\ell}))$. 
\end{itemize}
The seller stores goods to insure herself against the possibility that no goods are available when a buyer arrives, in which case the buyer either incurs a waiting cost or leaves unassigned. When more goods are in the queue, this possibility becomes less likely, and thus the marginal benefit of storing an additional good is lower. $L^*$ is the cutoff beyond which the marginal value is not worth the additional cost of storage.

The following result is an analogue of \cref{thm:optimal-policy}.
\begin{customthm}{$\mathbf{2'}$}\label{thm:optimal-policy-storable}
    The Markov policy $M^{*}$ implements the solution to the relaxed program $[\widetilde{\ms P}]$ and is therefore optimal.
\end{customthm}







\section{Implementation in Dominant Strategies and Periodic Ex Post Equilibrium}

The direct mechanism described in \cref{thm:optimal-policy-storable} is optimal within all Bayesian incentive-compatible mechanisms.  We can strengthen the robustness of the optimal mechanism in a couple of ways.

\subsection{Dominant Strategy Implementation} \label{sec:DSIC}

The optimal policy can be made dominant-strategy incentive-compatible (DSIC) via a realistic mechanism that combines monopoly pricing and standard auctions. The mechanism reimburses each buyer for the waiting cost he incurs and keeps the buyers' reported values (or bids) private from other buyers. (However, buyers may be informed about the queue size.)
The following mechanism, called {\bf cutoff price auctions}, is described as follows: 

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Suppose a buyer arrives when there is a positive inventory $\ell>0$ of goods available.  Then, the seller charges a {\bf monopoly price} of $\hat v_{-\ell}$.
    \item Buyers who join the queue participate in multiple auctions. A buyer remains \textbf{active} until they play the first assignment auction. From then on, a buyer becomes \textbf{passive} and must submit in each subsequent auction a fixed bid that is equal to the bid he submitted in the assignment auction.
    \item Suppose a buyer arrives when there is a queue of $k-1$ buyers and there are no goods in inventory.\footnote{Recall that our ``no allocation delay'' assumption precludes the possibility that the buyer queue and good inventory are both nonempty.}  Then, a {\bf survival auction} is conducted in which a common clock price rises continuously from zero. When a buyer drops out or the price reaches $\hat v_k$, the auction concludes---all remaining buyers stay in the queue, and for each such buyer, the final price is recorded as a reserve price for this survival auction. An active buyer may drop out any time, whereas a passive buyer must drop out at his fixed bid.
    \item Suppose a good arrives when the buyer queue is non-empty. Then, an {\bf assignment auction}  is held in which each active buyer in the queue makes a sealed bid in $\mathbb{R}_+$ no less than the highest reserve price recorded for that buyer, and each passive buyer bids his fixed bid.\footnote{An ascending English auction can be equivalently used, with the starting price chosen at the highest personal reserve price among all bidders.} 
    The highest bidder wins the auction and pays his  {\bf cutoff price}, which is defined to be {\it the lowest bid the bidder could have made in the assignment auction in which he won, assuming that he had only participated in the past survival auctions but not in the past assignment auctions (so that he is an active bidder in this auction), such that he would still have  ``eventually'' won the good.}
    Note that the cutoff price depends on the events unfolding after the assignment.\footnote{Therefore, strictly speaking, the payment rule is no longer Markovian. We present in \cref{subsec:periodic-EPIC} an alternative payment rule that restores the Markov property.} All active buyers become passive.
\end{enumerate}



The following example illustrates how the cutoff price is determined.  Assume $\hat v_1=1, \hat v_2=2$, and $\hat v_3=4$. Suppose the buyer queue and the inventory are initially both empty, and then buyer $A$ (with value $v_A=6$), buyer $B$ (with value $v_B=3$), and an item sequentially arrive, as depicted in \cref{figure:DSIC-example}. 
  Assuming truthful bidding, the survival clock stops at $\hat v_1=1$ when buyer $A$ arrives, and stops at $\hat v_2=2$ when buyer $B$ arrives. Both buyers survive and stay in the queue. When the item arrives, an assignment auction is held. Suppose both bid truthfully. Then, buyer $A$ wins, and the cutoff price he pays depends on what happens next:
\begin{itemize}
    \item In scenario 1 (the top branch in \cref{figure:DSIC-example}), a second item arrives. The cutoff price for buyer $A$ is his highest reserve price 2, since any bid higher than 2 will have secured the win of the first item (if it is higher than $3$) or the second item (if it is below 3). Likewise, buyer $B$ wins the second item and pays 2 after the arrival of the second item. 

    \item In scenario 2 (the bottom branch in \cref{figure:DSIC-example}), buyers $C$ and $D$ arrive with values $v_C=v_D=5$.  Upon the arrival of buyer $D$, buyer $B$ will lose the survival auction, dropping out at the price of 3, where the clock stops.  In this scenario, the cutoff price for buyer $A$ is 3, as any higher bid will continue to win, but any lower bid will lose the survival auction upon buyer $D$'s arrival.
\end{itemize}  



