Whenever the auction has multiple buyers, the cutoff price is determined after events unfold sufficiently to allow the seller to ``run a counterfactual'' of lowering the winning buyer's bid until the bidder does not win the good in the realized sample path.\footnote{There is a reasonable upper bound for the length of sample path required for determining the cutoff price.  Every time the queue empties (which occurs in finite expected time), the cutoff prices for all winning buyers before that point can be determined.}  In this case, the winner will be ``billed'' after the assignment.\footnote{Note that the billing need not be delayed if there is only one bidder when the good becomes available. In scenario 1 above, buyer $B$'s cutoff price is known when he receives the good.} 

 Importantly, the cutoff prices harness an important feature of our mechanism, namely, it leverages the future level of competition to discipline the current buyers. When the assignment is followed by an improved supply condition (as in scenario 1), this lowers the price charged to the winner. However, if excess demand arises (as in scenario 2), this future competition is leveraged into a higher price for the winner. 

In general, the cutoff price for the winning bidder is bounded above by the second-highest bid in that auction and below by the highest reserve price that the winning buyer has survived before.  Any bid above the second-highest bid will surely win, which can be seen in the first scenario.





 The auction rules guarantee that, in every possible circumstance, the winner gets the ``best possible deal'' compatible with winning. This ensures that there is no hindsight regret and makes the mechanism strategyproof. 

\begin{thm} \label{thm:DSIC}
The cutoff-price auctions mechanism specified above is strategyproof.  That is, it is a dominant strategy to (i) accept the good in (a) if and only if one's value is above the monopoly price, (ii) drop out of the survival auction in (c) if and only if the clock price has reached one's value, and (iii) bid one's valuation in the assignment auction in (d).
\end{thm}  

The dominance property of the mechanism makes it robust to the beliefs that participants have about the opponents' behaviors and the underlying stochastic environments.  In particular, the buyers' incentives for truthful reporting and their obedience are preserved even if the underlying process is off from the stationary distribution or if their beliefs about the states are misspecified.\footnote{For example, if the queue starts from $k=\ell=0$ at time zero and the buyers who arrive subsequently observe the calendar time, then the Markov chain under the optimal policy will not have converged to the stationary distribution.  Still, our buyers will have no incentive to report untruthfully or disobey the recommended behavior. Therefore, the long-run (time-average) revenue converges to the optimal level.}  This gives a much stronger performance guarantee not seen in the papers that adopt a steady-state mechanism design.  

To argue formally, suppose the time is now $t\in [0,\infty)$, with an arbitrary starting state $\omega_0\in \mathbf{V}\times \mathbb{Z}_+$ at time $t=0$.  Suppose our DSIC mechanism is used for $t\ge 0$.  Let  $(\Omega, \mathcal F, \{\mathcal F_t\}_{t=0}^{\infty}, \mathbb{P})$ denote the associated filtered probability space, where $\Omega$ is the set of all possible sample paths of the queue states $(\mathbf{V}\times \mathbb{Z}_+)^{[0,\infty)}$ with initial state $\omega_0$, which may arise from the mechanism,  $\mathcal F$ is the $\sigma$-algebra generated by the sample path, and $\{\mathcal F_t\}$ are filtrations generated by $\{\omega_t\}$.  Let $\omega_T$ denote a sample path up to time $T\ge 0$ and $R(\omega_T)$ denote the revenue generated up to that point.  We refer to this as the {\bf nonstationary model} with initial state $\omega_0$.

\begin{cor}  Buyers have a dominant strategy to behave truthfully and obey the recommendation under the DSIC mechanism in the nonstationary model with any arbitrary initial state $\omega_0$.  The long-run time-average of the revenue
$ R( \omega_T)/T$
converges almost surely to the value of $[\widetilde{\mathcal P}]$ as $T\to \infty$, regardless of the initial state $\omega_0\in  \mathbf{V}\times \mathbb{Z}_+$.  
\end{cor}

\begin{proof}  The first statement follows from the strategyproofness of the DSIC mechanism.  The second statement follows from adapting the strong law of large numbers for the positive recurrent regenerative process.  Let $\tau_i$, $i=1, ...$, denote the renewal times at which the process hits the empty queue state $\mathbf{0}\in \mathbf{V}\times \mathbb{Z}_+$. Let $\tilde R_i:=R(\omega_{\tau_{i+1}})-R(\omega_{\tau_{i}})$ be the revenue generated between times $\tau_i$ and $\tau_{i+1}$.  Letting $N_T:=\max\{n\in \mathbb{Z}_+: \tau_n \le T\} $, write
\begin{align*}
    \frac{R (\omega_T)}{T}&=\frac{R(\omega_{\tau_1})+\sum_{i=1}^{N_T}\tilde R_i + R(\omega_T)- R(\omega_{\tau_{N_T}})}{T}  \\
    &=\frac{N_T}{T} \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N_T} \tilde R_i}{N_T} + \frac{N_T}{T} \frac{R(\omega_{\tau_1})+ R(\omega_T)- R(\omega_{\tau_{N_T}})}{N_T}.
\end{align*}
Since $\tau_i$ is a renewal time, $\frac{N_T}{T}$ converges almost surely to $\frac{1}{\E[\tau_1]}$, which is positive since the process is positive recurrent. The latter also means that $N_T\to \infty$ almost surely.  Further, since our queue state process is regenerative, the random variables $\{\tilde R_i\}_{i=1}^{N_T}$ are iid. Furthermore, $ \E[|\tilde R_i|]\le \max\{ \mu,  cK\} \E[\tau_1]<\infty$.  Hence, by the strong law of large numbers, $\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N_T} \tilde R_i}{N_T}$ converges almost surely to $\E[\tilde R_1]$.  It is routine to prove that $\frac{R(\omega_{\tau_1})+ R(\omega_T)- R(\omega_{\tau_{N_T}})}{N_T}$ vanishes almost surely.  We thus conclude that $ \frac{R (\omega_T)}{T}$ converges almost surely to its mean $\frac{\E[\tilde R_1]}{\E[\tau_1]}$, regardless of $\omega_0$. Since the mean coincides with the value of $[\widetilde{\mathcal P}]$ when $\omega_0$ is distributed according to the stationary distribution $P^*$, the statement follows. 
\end{proof}

\subsection{Periodic Ex Post Implementation}\label{subsec:periodic-EPIC}

One feature of our dominant-strategy implementation is the possible delay of payments.  While delayed payments are not unrealistic and the possible enforcement issue can be addressed,\footnote{It is common for customers to be billed for later payments after the service is rendered.  Enforcement is possible by legal liability or the requirement for buyers to post bonds, which they recover after fulfilling the payment duty.} one may wonder if such a delay can be avoided.  Our original version of the direct mechanism with a lump-sum payment collected at the time of each buyer's arrival involves no delay and is Bayesian incentive compatible.  In fact, a delayed payment can be avoided while retaining some robustness property.  

Consider the same Markov policy in \Cref{sec:DSIC}, except that the winner at the assignment auction is charged the {\bf expected cutoff price} at the time of assignment, where the expectation is taken over the future arrival of items and buyers and their valuations, {\it assuming that all future buyers report truthfully.}  This mechanism, called the {\bf expected cutoff auction},
 is no longer dominant strategy incentive compatible but still stronger than Bayesian incentive compatible.  In fact, the mechanism satisfies the following notion, which \cite{bergemann2010dynamic} introduced for their dynamic pivot mechanism.  

\begin{defn} A (direct) mechanism is {\bf periodic ex post incentive compatible} if truth-telling is a best response for each agent, regardless of the history and the current state of the queue,  
given that all other agents report truthfully in the future.\footnote{To be precise, the periodic ex-post incentive compatibility, as defined by \cite{bergemann2010dynamic}, does not require dominance for the current period; in this sense, our mechanism is stronger. }    
\end{defn}

The qualifier ``periodic'' requires that the buyer forms a correct belief about the future state when making his bids.



\begin{cor}  The expected cutoff price auction is periodic ex post incentive compatible.  
\end{cor}
\begin{proof}  Since the cutoff price is dominant strategy incentive compatible for each state for all possible behavior by future buyers, its expectation over future states (including valuations of the future buyers) preserves incentives for truthful reporting so long as the future buyers report truthfully and a given buyer forms accurate beliefs about the future states.  
\end{proof}








\section{Large Market Properties}\label{sec:large-market}

Next, we investigate the large market properties of our optimal mechanism.  Specifically, we ``thicken'' the market density by letting $\l,\m\to\infty$ with the balance parameter $\rho=\l/\m$ held constant.  We show that the optimal mechanism converges to the familiar multiunit auction with the monopoly reserve price (that does not vary with the number of bidders).  Moreover, this is still true even if one of the two cost parameters, $c$ or $d$, also tends to $\infty$. These results will imply that as the market grows large, our assumption of no allocation delay entails no appreciable loss. 

\paragraph*{Oracle Benchmark.}

Consider the hypothetical setting where there are $n$ buyers, each with value drawn independently from $F$, competing for $m$ units of the good. Under the standard regularity condition, the $m+1$-th price uniform auction with a reserve price set at $\hat v_0:=J^{-1}(0)$ maximizes revenue.  The auction admits a dominant strategy of truthful bidding, so the selling price becomes the $m+1$-th highest valuation (set equal to zero if $m\ge n$) or the monopoly price $\hat v_0:=J^{-1}(0)$, whichever is higher:
$$\max\{v^{(m+1)}, \hat  v_0\}.$$
Let $R^*(m,n)$ denote the auction's expected revenue.

We argue that this outcome gives the seller an upper bound of the expected revenue to attain in our model in the limit. To this end, consider the following two-step process of constructing our stochastic model:  (i) build a primary sample path of buyer and item arrival according to $(\l,\m)$ rates; (ii) for each sample path, we draw the value of each arrived buyer according to $F$.  We can index the primary sample path in which $n$ buyers and $m$ items arrive during a unit interval of time by $(m,n)$ and let $R_{M}(m,n)$ denote the expected revenue resulting from such a sample path associated with some feasible Markovian policy $(M,t,\iota)$.  



\begin{lem}\label{lemma:large-market-1}
    Consider any primary sample path $(m,n)$ and the expected revenue $R_{M}(m,n)$ resulting from a feasible Markovian policy $(M,t,\iota)$.  Then, $R_{M}(m,n)\le R^*(m,n)$.
\end{lem} 



Now suppose we let $n,m \to \infty$ such that $n/m\to \rho:=\l/\m$. Then, the selling price converges a.s. to
$$\tilde v_0:=\max\{\tilde v, \hat  v_0\},$$
where 
$$\tilde v:=\inf\{v: \rho [1-F(v)]\le 1 \},$$  
and the seller's per-unit revenue $R^*(m,n)/m$ converges a.s. to
$$R^*:=\rho[1-F(\tilde v_0)]\tilde v_0.$$

Let $\mathcal M$ be the set of all feasible Markovian policies. One can show:

\begin{lem} \label{lemma:large-market-2}
The expected per-unit revenue achieved by any feasible Markovian policy is no greater than $R^*$ in the limit as $\l,\m\to \infty$ with $\rho=\l/\m$ held constant.
\end{lem}

We now present our main result for this section.

\begin{thm}\label{thm:large-market}
The expected per-unit revenue obtained by the optimal Markovian policy converges to the oracle benchmark $R^*$ if (a)
 $\l,\m\to \infty$ with $\rho=\l/\m$ held constant, or (b) $c \to 0$, or (c) $d \to 0$. 
\end{thm}

Note that multiplying the four parameters $\l,\m,c$, and $d$ by a constant $k > 0$ is equivalent to simply rescaling time.
Therefore, (b) is equivalent to $\l,\m,d \to \infty$ with $\l/\m$ and $\l/d$ held constant, and (c) is equivalent to $\l,\m,c \to \infty$ with $\l/\m$ and $\l/c$ held constant. Thus (a) is implied by either (b) or (c).

To prove (b),  consider a simple  Markovian policy which assigns an incoming good to a waiting buyer if the buyer queue is non-empty and discards the good otherwise, and queues a buyer if and only if his value is above $\tilde v_0 + \d$ for an arbitrarily small $\d>0$ (see \cref{fig:large-market}). This ensures that the service rate $\mu$ is larger than the ``effective arrival rate'' $\l[1-F(\tilde v_0 + \d)]$.  Then, every buyer with a value above $\tilde v_0 + \d$ is eventually assigned a good after a delay, but the waiting costs associated with the delay can be shown to vanish as $c\to 0$.  Therefore, the per-unit revenue under this simple policy converges to the upper bound $R^*$. This implies that the revenue from our optimal policy must also converge to $R^*$.  

Similarly, to prove (c), we consider a mechanism which sells a stored item to an incoming buyer at price $\tilde v_0+\delta$, turns away buyers when inventory is empty, and stores at most $\bar L:=\sqrt{\mu/d}$ items. We show that, as $d\to 0$, both the average storage cost borne by the designer and the probability of stockout (conditional on buyer arrival) vanish.

These large market results suggest that NAD is without loss as the market grows large or queueing costs on either side become small. This is because even if we did not impose NAD, the per-unit revenue would not be able to exceed the oracle benchmark $R^*$. 


