\section{Welfare maximization}

We have focused on maximizing the seller's revenue, but our analysis can easily be extended to solve for a mechanism that maximizes a weighted sum of the seller's revenue and the buyer's welfare. Suppose a social planner places a weight of 1 on the seller's payoff and a weight of $w \leq 1$ on the buyer's payoff. Then, all one needs to do is to replace $J(v)$ in the relaxed program $[\widetilde{\mathcal P}]$ with\footnote{To see this, note that the seller's objective function becomes
\begin{align*}
    &\, \l\int_0^1 \big[w (vX(v) - T(v) - cW(v) ) + T(v) \big] f(v) \ddd v - d \sum_{\ell=1}^L \ell q_\ell\\
    = &\,\l\int_0^1 \big[ vX(v) - c W(v) - (1-w)\int_0^v X(s) \ddd s - (1-w)U(0) \big] f(v) \ddd v - d \sum_{\ell=1}^L \ell q_\ell.
\end{align*}
For $w \leq 1$, it is optimal to set $U(0) = 0$, so we simply replace $J(v)$ with $J_w(v)$. For $w > 1$, if one introduces an ex ante IR constraint for the seller, then it becomes optimal to maximize the sum of the buyers' and the seller's payoffs and have the seller transfer her expected revenue to the buyers.
}
$$ J_w(v) \:= v - (1-w)\frac{1-F(v)}{f(v)}.$$
As a result, the welfare-maximizing policy has the same type of threshold structure as the revenue-maximizing policy.
When $w=1$, we have $J_w(v)=v$, so that there is no distortion arising from the buyers' incentive constraints.

\begin{prop}
    Given a Pareto weight of $w \in[0,1]$ on the buyers, welfare is maximized by changing the thresholds of the optimal policy to some $(\hat v_{-L^w},\ldots,\hat v_{-1}^w,\hat v_1^w, \ldots, \hat v_{K_w}^w)$.
\end{prop}

Given that the thresholds in our optimal policy play a role similar to that of the reserve price in a static auction, one might have expected that a higher weight on the buyers' utilities should lead to a lower threshold. Focusing for simplicity on the case without storage ($d=\infty$), the following proposition states that this is only true when the queue is relatively short. When the queue is already long, a social planner who places a larger weight on the buyers' utilities is \textit{more} selective in admitting a new buyer into a queue.

\begin{prop}[Comparative Statics in $w$] \label{prop:comp-stat-in-pareto-weight} Suppose $d=\infty$, and suppose the inverse hazard rate $(1-F(v))/f(v)$ is non-increasing. For any Pareto weights $w,\tilde w \in [0,1]$ with $w > \tilde w$, there exist $\bar k \in \{1,2,\ldots,\min\{K_{\tilde w}, K_w\}\}$ such that $\hat v^w_k \leq \hat v^{\tilde w}_k$ for $k \leq \bar k$, and $\hat v^w_k > \hat v^{\tilde w}_k$ for $k \geq \bar k+1$.
\end{prop}
\cref{prop:comp-stat-in-pareto-weight} says that a single-crossing property holds: if $w>w'$, the thresholds are initially lower under $w$ when the queue is short, but they increase more quickly in queue length and may eventually overtake the thresholds under $w'$ (see \cref{fig:comp-stat-w}). 
To see why, recall from \eqref{eq51} that the first threshold $\hat v_1^w$ must be decreasing in $w$ because $J_w(v)$ is increasing in $w$. At the same time, \eqref{eq51} implies that the distance between two adjacent thresholds must be decreasing in $\frac{P_1^*(v)}{P_k^*(0)}J_w'(v)$. $J_w'(v)$ is decreasing in $w$ given the monotone hazard rate assumption, so $\frac{P_1^*(v)}{P_k^*(0)}J_w'(v)$ is decreasing in $w$. Hence, a higher Pareto weight $w$ implies that the thresholds increase faster.

That the derivative of the virtual value $J_w'(v)$ is decreasing in $w$ represents the fact that a buyer's information rent is decreasing in type. Intuitively, suppose we compare the optimal thresholds chosen by a welfare-maximizing planner ($w=1$) against those chosen by a revenue-maximizing seller ($w=0$). 
Maintaining a queue of buyers provides a protection against two related but distinct types of losses: the loss from the buyer queue being empty when a good arrives, in which case the good is wasted, and the loss from the queue only containing low-valued buyers when a good arrives, in which case the good is not allocated to a high-valued buyer. The first type of loss is more costly for welfare than for revenue, since the virtual value of a buyer ($J_0(v) = J(v)$) is always lower than his actual value ($J_1(v) = v$). 
On the other hand, the second type of loss is more costly to a revenue-maximizing seller, since high-valued buyers also come with low information rent. When the queue length is relatively short, the first type of loss is more likely to occur, so the planner is more willing to admit buyers than is the seller. When the queue length is long, the second type of loss is more likely, so the planner is more selective in admitting buyers.

There is a second, complementary force contributing to the single-crossing property. Because $\frac{P_1^*(v)}{P_k^*(0)}$ is non-decreasing in $v$, the distance between two adjacent thresholds, $\hat v_k$ and $\hat v_{k+1}$, tends to be longer when $\hat v_k$ is lower. Intuitively, when the seller is more permissive in admitting the first buyer into the queue, there is less risk of goods arriving when there are no buyers, so there is less benefit from admitting a second buyer.

