 \section{Conclusion}

Motivated by applications such as cloud computing, gig platforms, and blockchain
auctions, we study the design of optimal selling mechanisms in a dynamic environment where buyers and goods arrive asynchronously and stochastically. We derive the optimal dynamic mechanism at steady state via an infinite-dimensional linear programming problem. 
The optimal mechanism, characterized by reserve prices that increase with queue length and auctions to allocate the goods,  efficiently manages the inventory of goods, the customer queue, and the competition between buyers. 



Our analysis can be extended in several directions.  First, we may generalize the service process beyond the M/M/1 structure, as considered by \cite{che2021optimal}.  Second, one may consider a model where, instead of goods simply becoming available, they are delivered by suppliers with heterogeneous and privately informed costs.  This gives rise to a {\it double auction} model.  Third, one can relax the assumption of homogeneous goods to allow for heterogeneity of goods.  While our model takes the first important step toward modeling the platform exchange setting, the heterogeneity of goods and services represents an important feature of platform exchanges.   These extensions, as well as other worthy ones, await future work.





















































