In this study, we employed two categories of classifiers: vector-based and image-based. 
% These approaches allowed us to compare traditional sequence vectorization techniques with advanced deep-learning models for image-based sequence classification.
In the vector-based setting, we use the standard nearest neighbor classifier, which is well-known and traditionally used in the literature for such tasks. 
For the image-based classifiers, we use various deep-learning architectures, including 1, 2, and 3 layers of Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), Visual Geometry Group (VGG19), ResNet50, EfficientNet, and DenseNet.
For the parameters of the DL models, we selected values for batch size, epochs, and learning rate as $64$, $10$, and $0.003$, respectively. For the optimizer, we use ADAM. For hyperparameter tuning, we use a standard cross-validation setting.
To evaluate the performance of the classifiers, we use average accuracy, precision, recall, weighted F1, Macro F1, ROC-AUC, and training runtime.

% a comprehensive set of metrics that measure both accuracy and generalization. Accuracy was calculated as the proportion of correct predictions out of the total number of predictions, providing an overall measure of the model’s correctness. However, since accuracy can be misleading for imbalanced datasets, we also considered precision, which measures the proportion of true positive predictions out of all positive predictions made by the model. This metric is essential in scenarios where minimizing false positives is important. Recall was employed to assess the model's ability to identify all relevant instances, calculated as the proportion of true positives out of all actual positives. A combination of these metrics is captured in the F1-Score, which provides a harmonic mean of precision and recall. We computed both Weighted F1-Score, which accounts for class imbalance by assigning weights based on class frequency, and Macro F1-Score, which gives equal weight to each class, regardless of their frequency. Additionally, we evaluated the ROC-AUC, which reflects the model’s ability to distinguish between classes by measuring the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve. Finally, we tracked the training runtime of classifiers, an important metric for assessing computational efficiency.



