\section{Hardware Experiments on IonQ's Forte-Generation Quantum Computers}
\label{sec:IonQsim}


%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Circuit Implementation and Performance Optimization}
\label{sec:implementation_and_optimization}
\noindent
Our quantum simulations first apply circuits to prepare the initial state
$\vert \psi^{(\text{lep})}_{\text{vac}} \rangle \, \vert\Delta^-\Delta^-\rangle $
on 32 qubits, 
and then implement two steps of first-order Trotterized time evolution.
To reduce the number of two-qubit gates, we implement several approximations in the time evolution.
As mentioned in Sec.~\ref{sec:Hami}, we truncate the chromoelectric interaction beyond $\lambda=1$ staggered sites and only keep the terms in $\hat{H}_{\beta}^{1+1}$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:HbetaC1}, that act on valence quarks and leptons.
Additionally two-qubit rotations with angles $\theta \leq t/32$ are removed.
For more details on the approximations used and their effects on the simulations
see Appendix~\ref{app:classical_simulations}.
After using IonQ's circuit compiler, the required circuits have 470 $R_{ZZ}$ gates.



To further optimize circuit performance, 
we used multiple noise tailoring, error mitigation and error detection methods. 
Details of each method are
given in Appendices~\ref{app:em_dnl} and \ref{app:em_flag}.
We apply Pauli twirling~\cite{Wallman:2015uzh}, 
XY4 dynamic decoupling~\cite{Viola:1998jx,Ezzell:2022uat} and measurement twirling through bit flipping~\cite{PhysRevA.105.032620,Smith_2021}.
These techniques mitigate coherent two-qubit over- and under-rotations, phase and idling errors, spontaneous emission and measurement biases.
Each circuit is compiled to pairs of twirled variants with unique qubit assignments to debias qubit-associated error dependencies. 
Each pair of variants is identical up to bit flips before and after measurement that symmetrize readout errors. 
A total of 96 twirled variants, each with 150 shots, was chosen to balance
the ability to mitigate (systematic) hardware errors by increasing the number of variants, and reducing statistical uncertainties by performing more shots per variant. For $t=2$, we included 64 additional variants, totaling 160 (24,000 shots total).
The tradeoff between number of twirled variants and shots per variant was informed by a Monte Carlo analysis over a uniform distribution of possible output bit string distributions, assuming the hardware error rates taken from benchmarking data. 
See Appendix~\ref{app:MCMethod} for details.




Our simulations only utilized 32 of the 36 qubits available on the Forte-generation machines.
The remaining four qubits were used to check for qubit leakage outside of the computational subspace. 
See Appendix~\ref{app:em_flag} for more information.
Measured bit strings are post selected based on the ancillae states indicating the absence of leakage.
Additionally bit strings are post-selected to conserve color and total electric charges.
The charge operators are given in Appendix~\ref{app:HamDetails}.




