\subsection{Results}
\noindent
Decays can be detected by measuring the total electric charge of the electrons $\hat{Q}_e$ and the lepton number $\hat{{\cal L}}$.
These observables are
%
\begin{align}
{\hat Q}_e & \ = \  - \frac{1}{2}\sum_{n=0}^3 \hat{Z}_{25+2n} \ ,  \nonumber \\ 
\hat{{\cal L}} & \ = \ \frac{1}{2}\sum_{n=0}^{3}\left (\hat{Z}_{24+2n} + \hat{Z}_{25+2n} \right ) \ .
\label{eq:qe_lnum}
\end{align}
%
Both the lepton electric charge and lepton number are zero in the initial state, and a non-zero lepton electric charge signals a decay. 
Further, deviations of the lepton number from zero are due to the neutrino Majorana mass.


