\paragraph{Feedback closed walk.}
Fix $i \in [2]$. A \emph{feedback closed walk} is a non-contractible weakly-simple closed walk~$C$ of $L_i(G)$ that
crosses all cycles in the dual of $L_i(G)$ and that visits each vertex of $L_i(G)$ at most twice.  
Define graph $L_i(G) \para C$ by \emph{splitting $L_i(G)$ along~$C$} as follows:

\textit{Case 1 -- $C$ is a cycle:}
Duplicate~$C$ into two copies~$C_s$ and~$C_t$;
for a vertex~$v \in V(C)$, let the copies be~$v_s$ and~$v_t$, respectively.
Distribute each edge incident to~$v$ but not on~$C$ accordingly to~$v_s$ and~$v_t$
based on whether it attaches to~$C$ at~$v$ from the right or the left, respectively.

\textit{Case 2 -- $C$ is weakly simple:}
Let $C[u,v]$ be a subpath of $C$ that visited twice; see~\cref{fig:torus:single:g}.
As before, duplicate $C$ into two copies~$C_s$ and~$C_t$ and distribute edges accordingly,
which results in three copies of each vertex on $C[u,v]$ instead of four copies; see~\cref{fig:torus:single:split}.
(Imaging cutting through a drawing of $L_i(G)$ with edges of non-zero thickness.
Then the cut moves through $C[u,v]$ twice, creating three copies.)  

Either way, graph $L_i(G) \para C$ is obtained by splitting a toroidal graph along a non-contractible curve, 
so it is cylindrical with its two outer faces incident to $C$
(the two outer faces share subpaths whenever $C$ visits such a path twice).
So if $C$ crosses all directed cycles of $L_i^\star(G)$, then the dual of~$L_i(G)\para C$ is acyclic.  
So a crucial step is how to find a feedback closed walk, preferably a simple and orbital~one.

