\subsubsection{Essentially self-adjointness}
We say a set $\mathcal C\subset D(L^{\sB, \sN, \lambda})$ is dense in the domain of the operator $L^{\sB, \sN, \lambda}$ if the domain $D(L^{\sB, \sN, \lambda})$ is the closure of $\mathcal C$ w.r.t. the graph norm 
$$\left\| u\right\|^2_{D(L^{\sB,\sN,\lambda})} = \left\| u\right\|_{L^2(\m_\sB^\sN)}^2 + \left\| L^{\sB, \sN, \lambda}u\right\|_{L^2(\m_\sB^\sN)}^2 . $$
The operator $L^{\sB, \sN, \lambda}|_{\mathcal C}$ restricted to $\mathcal C$ is called essentially self-adjoint if it has a unique  self-adjoint extension. 

It  is a general fact about essentially self-adjoint operators that in  this case $\mathcal C$ is dense w.r.t. $\left\|\cdot \right\|_{D(L^{\sB,\sN,\lambda})}$ in the domain of this extension. 
