\end{figure}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
In this limit, the binding energy $\frac{U}{2}N(N-1)$ is equal for all soliton solutions and will be disregarded. Transport of the composite object occurs through collective hopping of individual particles, arising in $N$th order perturbation theory, which has a very small effective amplitude of $\sim J^N/U^{N-1}$ \cite{Mila_2010}. At the same time, virtual hopping processes of particles from the soliton site to a neighboring site and back give rise to local energy shifts with an amplitude proportional to $\sim J^2/U$.
As shown in detail in the method section \hyperref[sec:effective_methods]{C}, this results in the effective energies and hopping amplitudes
%
\begin{equation}
    \epsilon_{l, \textrm{eff}} = \frac{3}{2} \frac{J_{l-1}^2 + J_l^2}{U},\qquad 
    J_{l,\textrm{eff}} = \frac{3}{2}\frac{J_l^3}{U^2}
    \label{eq:triplon}
\end{equation}
%
for the triplon model (i.e. $N=3$). (For effective Bloch hamiltonians of bound doublons and their topological features see also \cite{valiente2008two,PhysRevLett.117.213603,huang2024topological}).
%
In Fig.~\ref{fig:triplon} we have shown the soliton energies of the effective triplon model for two different interaction strengths for the AAH model along with the Wilson-loop ${\cal W}_m=\textrm{Im}\log \det \mathbf{P}(t)$, see Eq.~\eqref{eq:wilson-loop}, where $m$ is the number of crossing bands.
One cleary sees that for increased interactions the
bands merge. Eventually, all $5$ bands cross and there is no winding of 
${\cal W}_5$, i.e. the 
total Wilson loop vanishes. Thus, despite the fact that the soliton mass is 
still finite, the topological transport vanishes exactly. 


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{summary and outlook}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

We developed a full quantum description of topological pumps of self-bound $N$-particle lattice solitons. The quantum description allowed us to identify and to explicitly calculate a many-body topological invariant, i. e., the Chern-number or Wilson-loop explaining the emergence of integer and fractional transport also in the full range of interaction strength, where perturbative arguments fail \cite{jurgensen2022chern,mostaan2022quantized}. Transitions between phases of differently quantized topological transport observed in experiments \cite{jurgensen2021quantized,jurgensen2023quantized} 
as well as the possibility of phases with breakdown of topological quantization were explained by coalescence of soliton bands and possible degeneracies with extended states. We believe that the concept can easily be extended to multi-component solitons \cite{PhysRevResearch.6.L042010,tao2024nonlinearity,tao2025nonlinearity}, were among other things fractional transport was predicted despite the fact that all single-particle bands are topologically trivial. In the latter case interactions not only lead to the modification of topological properties, such as the transition from integer to fractional phases, but to the \textit{emergence} of topology. 

