The main achievement of our work is that by translating the categorical Grothendieck construction into the theory of computability models, we manage to bridge the categorical semantics of dependent type theory with higher-order computability. Concepts and results from Martin-L\"of type theory acquire a general categorical formulation within the theory of categories with family arrows and $\Sigma$-objects. Dependent functions are formulated categorically within the theory of categories with dependent arrows and the second projection associared to the dependent-pair type is translated as a certain dependent arrow. By proving that the category of computability models is a (fam, $\Sigma$)-category, and also a type-category, in the sense of Pitts, these concepts and results are translated into the theory of computability models. In this way, we propose a new direction to the solution of the problem of combining constructive type theory with classical approaches to functional algorithms, as this was formulated by Longley and Normann.

